window.SHAKESPEARE_ENGLISH["allswell"] = {"id":"allswell","title":"All's Well That Ends Well","kind":"play","source":"MIT Shakespeare (Complete Moby Shakespeare)","sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/allswell/full.html","sourceRevision":"6b82db852c7322dd33e95db347f0ddfc812c6409","sections":[{"id":"1.1","label":"Act 1 · SCENE I. Rousillon. The COUNT's palace.","act":1,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/allswell/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter BERTRAM, the COUNTESS of Rousillon, HELENA, and LAFEU, all in black","id":"allswell:1.1:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"In delivering my son from me, I bury a second husband.","sourceLine":"1.1.1","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:1.1:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And I in going, madam, weep o'er my father's death\nanew: but I must attend his majesty's command, to\nwhom I am now in ward, evermore in subjection.","sourceLine":"1.1.2","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:1.1:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You shall find of the king a husband, madam; you,\nsir, a father: he that so generally is at all times\ngood must of necessity hold his virtue to you; whose\nworthiness would stir it up where it wanted rather\nthan lack it where there is such abundance.","sourceLine":"1.1.5","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:1.1:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What hope is there of his majesty's amendment?","sourceLine":"1.1.10","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:1.1:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He hath abandoned his physicians, madam; under whose\npractises he hath persecuted time with hope, and\nfinds no other advantage in the process but only the\nlosing of hope by time.","sourceLine":"1.1.11","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:1.1:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"This young gentlewoman had a father,--O, that\n'had'! how sad a passage 'tis!--whose skill was\nalmost as great as his honesty; had it stretched so\nfar, would have made nature immortal, and death\nshould have play for lack of work. Would, for the\nking's sake, he were living! I think it would be\nthe death of the king's disease.","sourceLine":"1.1.15","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:1.1:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How called you the man you speak of, madam?","sourceLine":"1.1.22","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:1.1:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He was famous, sir, in his profession, and it was\nhis great right to be so: Gerard de Narbon.","sourceLine":"1.1.23","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:1.1:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He was excellent indeed, madam: the king very\nlately spoke of him admiringly and mourningly: he\nwas skilful enough to have lived still, if knowledge\ncould be set up against mortality.","sourceLine":"1.1.25","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:1.1:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What is it, my good lord, the king languishes of?","sourceLine":"1.1.29","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:1.1:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A fistula, my lord.","sourceLine":"1.1.30","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:1.1:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I heard not of it before.","sourceLine":"1.1.31","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:1.1:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I would it were not notorious. Was this gentlewoman\nthe daughter of Gerard de Narbon?","sourceLine":"1.1.32","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:1.1:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"His sole child, my lord, and bequeathed to my\noverlooking. I have those hopes of her good that\nher education promises; her dispositions she\ninherits, which makes fair gifts fairer; for where\nan unclean mind carries virtuous qualities, there\ncommendations go with pity; they are virtues and\ntraitors too; in her they are the better for their\nsimpleness; she derives her honesty and achieves her goodness.","sourceLine":"1.1.34","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:1.1:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Your commendations, madam, get from her tears.","sourceLine":"1.1.42","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:1.1:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'Tis the best brine a maiden can season her praise\nin. The remembrance of her father never approaches\nher heart but the tyranny of her sorrows takes all\nlivelihood from her cheek. No more of this, Helena;\ngo to, no more; lest it be rather thought you affect\na sorrow than have it.","sourceLine":"1.1.43","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:1.1:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I do affect a sorrow indeed, but I have it too.","sourceLine":"1.1.49","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:1.1:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Moderate lamentation is the right of the dead,\nexcessive grief the enemy to the living.","sourceLine":"1.1.50","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:1.1:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If the living be enemy to the grief, the excess\nmakes it soon mortal.","sourceLine":"1.1.52","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:1.1:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Madam, I desire your holy wishes.","sourceLine":"1.1.54","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:1.1:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How understand we that?","sourceLine":"1.1.55","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:1.1:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Be thou blest, Bertram, and succeed thy father\nIn manners, as in shape! thy blood and virtue\nContend for empire in thee, and thy goodness\nShare with thy birthright! Love all, trust a few,\nDo wrong to none: be able for thine enemy\nRather in power than use, and keep thy friend\nUnder thy own life's key: be cheque'd for silence,\nBut never tax'd for speech. What heaven more will,\nThat thee may furnish and my prayers pluck down,\nFall on thy head! Farewell, my lord;\n'Tis an unseason'd courtier; good my lord,\nAdvise him.","sourceLine":"1.1.56","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:1.1:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He cannot want the best\nThat shall attend his love.","sourceLine":"1.1.68","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:1.1:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Heaven bless him! Farewell, Bertram.","sourceLine":"1.1.70","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:1.1:25"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"allswell:1.1:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[To HELENA] The best wishes that can be forged in\nyour thoughts be servants to you! Be comfortable\nto my mother, your mistress, and make much of her.","sourceLine":"1.1.71","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:1.1:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Farewell, pretty lady: you must hold the credit of\nyour father.","sourceLine":"1.1.74","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:1.1:28"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt BERTRAM and LAFEU","id":"allswell:1.1:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, were that all! I think not on my father;\nAnd these great tears grace his remembrance more\nThan those I shed for him. What was he like?\nI have forgot him: my imagination\nCarries no favour in't but Bertram's.\nI am undone: there is no living, none,\nIf Bertram be away. 'Twere all one\nThat I should love a bright particular star\nAnd think to wed it, he is so above me:\nIn his bright radiance and collateral light\nMust I be comforted, not in his sphere.\nThe ambition in my love thus plagues itself:\nThe hind that would be mated by the lion\nMust die for love. 'Twas pretty, though plague,\nTo see him every hour; to sit and draw\nHis arched brows, his hawking eye, his curls,\nIn our heart's table; heart too capable\nOf every line and trick of his sweet favour:\nBut now he's gone, and my idolatrous fancy\nMust sanctify his reliques. Who comes here?","sourceLine":"1.1.76","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:1.1:30"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter PAROLLES","id":"allswell:1.1:31"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Aside","id":"allswell:1.1:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"One that goes with him: I love him for his sake;\nAnd yet I know him a notorious liar,\nThink him a great way fool, solely a coward;\nYet these fixed evils sit so fit in him,\nThat they take place, when virtue's steely bones\nLook bleak i' the cold wind: withal, full oft we see\nCold wisdom waiting on superfluous folly.","sourceLine":"1.1.96","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:1.1:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Save you, fair queen!","sourceLine":"1.1.103","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:1.1:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And you, monarch!","sourceLine":"1.1.104","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:1.1:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No.","sourceLine":"1.1.105","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:1.1:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And no.","sourceLine":"1.1.106","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:1.1:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Are you meditating on virginity?","sourceLine":"1.1.107","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:1.1:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay. You have some stain of soldier in you: let me\nask you a question. Man is enemy to virginity; how\nmay we barricado it against him?","sourceLine":"1.1.108","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:1.1:39"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Keep him out.","sourceLine":"1.1.111","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:1.1:40"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But he assails; and our virginity, though valiant,\nin the defence yet is weak: unfold to us some\nwarlike resistance.","sourceLine":"1.1.112","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:1.1:41"},{"kind":"speech","text":"There is none: man, sitting down before you, will\nundermine you and blow you up.","sourceLine":"1.1.115","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:1.1:42"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Bless our poor virginity from underminers and\nblowers up! Is there no military policy, how\nvirgins might blow up men?","sourceLine":"1.1.117","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:1.1:43"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Virginity being blown down, man will quicklier be\nblown up: marry, in blowing him down again, with\nthe breach yourselves made, you lose your city. It\nis not politic in the commonwealth of nature to\npreserve virginity. Loss of virginity is rational\nincrease and there was never virgin got till\nvirginity was first lost. That you were made of is\nmetal to make virgins. Virginity by being once lost\nmay be ten times found; by being ever kept, it is\never lost: 'tis too cold a companion; away with 't!","sourceLine":"1.1.120","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:1.1:44"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I will stand for 't a little, though therefore I die a virgin.","sourceLine":"1.1.130","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:1.1:45"},{"kind":"speech","text":"There's little can be said in 't; 'tis against the\nrule of nature. To speak on the part of virginity,\nis to accuse your mothers; which is most infallible\ndisobedience. He that hangs himself is a virgin:\nvirginity murders itself and should be buried in\nhighways out of all sanctified limit, as a desperate\noffendress against nature. Virginity breeds mites,\nmuch like a cheese; consumes itself to the very\nparing, and so dies with feeding his own stomach.\nBesides, virginity is peevish, proud, idle, made of\nself-love, which is the most inhibited sin in the\ncanon. Keep it not; you cannot choose but loose\nby't: out with 't! within ten year it will make\nitself ten, which is a goodly increase; and the\nprincipal itself not much the worse: away with 't!","sourceLine":"1.1.131","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:1.1:46"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How might one do, sir, to lose it to her own liking?","sourceLine":"1.1.146","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:1.1:47"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let me see: marry, ill, to like him that ne'er it\nlikes. 'Tis a commodity will lose the gloss with\nlying; the longer kept, the less worth: off with 't\nwhile 'tis vendible; answer the time of request.\nVirginity, like an old courtier, wears her cap out\nof fashion: richly suited, but unsuitable: just\nlike the brooch and the tooth-pick, which wear not\nnow. Your date is better in your pie and your\nporridge than in your cheek; and your virginity,\nyour old virginity, is like one of our French\nwithered pears, it looks ill, it eats drily; marry,\n'tis a withered pear; it was formerly better;\nmarry, yet 'tis a withered pear: will you anything with it?","sourceLine":"1.1.147","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:1.1:48"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Not my virginity yet [ ]\nThere shall your master have a thousand loves,\nA mother and a mistress and a friend,\nA phoenix, captain and an enemy,\nA guide, a goddess, and a sovereign,\nA counsellor, a traitress, and a dear;\nHis humble ambition, proud humility,\nHis jarring concord, and his discord dulcet,\nHis faith, his sweet disaster; with a world\nOf pretty, fond, adoptious christendoms,\nThat blinking Cupid gossips. Now shall he--\nI know not what he shall. God send him well!\nThe court's a learning place, and he is one--","sourceLine":"1.1.160","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:1.1:49"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What one, i' faith?","sourceLine":"1.1.173","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:1.1:50"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That I wish well. 'Tis pity--","sourceLine":"1.1.174","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:1.1:51"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What's pity?","sourceLine":"1.1.175","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:1.1:52"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That wishing well had not a body in't,\nWhich might be felt; that we, the poorer born,\nWhose baser stars do shut us up in wishes,\nMight with effects of them follow our friends,\nAnd show what we alone must think, which never\nReturn us thanks.","sourceLine":"1.1.176","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:1.1:53"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Page","id":"allswell:1.1:54"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Monsieur Parolles, my lord calls for you.","sourceLine":"1.1.182","speaker":"Page","id":"allswell:1.1:55"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"allswell:1.1:56"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Little Helen, farewell; if I can remember thee, I\nwill think of thee at court.","sourceLine":"1.1.183","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:1.1:57"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Monsieur Parolles, you were born under a charitable star.","sourceLine":"1.1.185","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:1.1:58"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Under Mars, I.","sourceLine":"1.1.186","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:1.1:59"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I especially think, under Mars.","sourceLine":"1.1.187","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:1.1:60"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why under Mars?","sourceLine":"1.1.188","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:1.1:61"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The wars have so kept you under that you must needs\nbe born under Mars.","sourceLine":"1.1.189","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:1.1:62"},{"kind":"speech","text":"When he was predominant.","sourceLine":"1.1.191","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:1.1:63"},{"kind":"speech","text":"When he was retrograde, I think, rather.","sourceLine":"1.1.192","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:1.1:64"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why think you so?","sourceLine":"1.1.193","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:1.1:65"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You go so much backward when you fight.","sourceLine":"1.1.194","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:1.1:66"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That's for advantage.","sourceLine":"1.1.195","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:1.1:67"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So is running away, when fear proposes the safety;\nbut the composition that your valour and fear makes\nin you is a virtue of a good wing, and I like the wear well.","sourceLine":"1.1.196","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:1.1:68"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I am so full of businesses, I cannot answer thee\nacutely. I will return perfect courtier; in the\nwhich, my instruction shall serve to naturalize\nthee, so thou wilt be capable of a courtier's\ncounsel and understand what advice shall thrust upon\nthee; else thou diest in thine unthankfulness, and\nthine ignorance makes thee away: farewell. When\nthou hast leisure, say thy prayers; when thou hast\nnone, remember thy friends; get thee a good husband,\nand use him as he uses thee; so, farewell.","sourceLine":"1.1.199","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:1.1:69"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"allswell:1.1:70"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Our remedies oft in ourselves do lie,\nWhich we ascribe to heaven: the fated sky\nGives us free scope, only doth backward pull\nOur slow designs when we ourselves are dull.\nWhat power is it which mounts my love so high,\nThat makes me see, and cannot feed mine eye?\nThe mightiest space in fortune nature brings\nTo join like likes and kiss like native things.\nImpossible be strange attempts to those\nThat weigh their pains in sense and do suppose\nWhat hath been cannot be: who ever strove\nSo show her merit, that did miss her love?\nThe king's disease--my project may deceive me,\nBut my intents are fix'd and will not leave me.","sourceLine":"1.1.209","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:1.1:71"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"allswell:1.1:72"}],"scene":1},{"id":"1.2","label":"Act 1 · SCENE II. Paris. The KING's palace.","act":1,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/allswell/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Flourish of cornets. Enter the KING of France, with letters, and divers Attendants","id":"allswell:1.2:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The Florentines and Senoys are by the ears;\nHave fought with equal fortune and continue\nA braving war.","sourceLine":"1.2.1","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:1.2:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So 'tis reported, sir.","sourceLine":"1.2.4","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:1.2:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, 'tis most credible; we here received it\nA certainty, vouch'd from our cousin Austria,\nWith caution that the Florentine will move us\nFor speedy aid; wherein our dearest friend\nPrejudicates the business and would seem\nTo have us make denial.","sourceLine":"1.2.5","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:1.2:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"His love and wisdom,\nApproved so to your majesty, may plead\nFor amplest credence.","sourceLine":"1.2.11","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:1.2:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He hath arm'd our answer,\nAnd Florence is denied before he comes:\nYet, for our gentlemen that mean to see\nThe Tuscan service, freely have they leave\nTo stand on either part.","sourceLine":"1.2.14","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:1.2:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"It well may serve\nA nursery to our gentry, who are sick\nFor breathing and exploit.","sourceLine":"1.2.19","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:1.2:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What's he comes here?","sourceLine":"1.2.22","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:1.2:8"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter BERTRAM, LAFEU, and PAROLLES","id":"allswell:1.2:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"It is the Count Rousillon, my good lord,\nYoung Bertram.","sourceLine":"1.2.23","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:1.2:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Youth, thou bear'st thy father's face;\nFrank nature, rather curious than in haste,\nHath well composed thee. Thy father's moral parts\nMayst thou inherit too! Welcome to Paris.","sourceLine":"1.2.25","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:1.2:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My thanks and duty are your majesty's.","sourceLine":"1.2.29","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:1.2:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I would I had that corporal soundness now,\nAs when thy father and myself in friendship\nFirst tried our soldiership! He did look far\nInto the service of the time and was\nDiscipled of the bravest: he lasted long;\nBut on us both did haggish age steal on\nAnd wore us out of act. It much repairs me\nTo talk of your good father. In his youth\nHe had the wit which I can well observe\nTo-day in our young lords; but they may jest\nTill their own scorn return to them unnoted\nEre they can hide their levity in honour;\nSo like a courtier, contempt nor bitterness\nWere in his pride or sharpness; if they were,\nHis equal had awaked them, and his honour,\nClock to itself, knew the true minute when\nException bid him speak, and at this time\nHis tongue obey'd his hand: who were below him\nHe used as creatures of another place\nAnd bow'd his eminent top to their low ranks,\nMaking them proud of his humility,\nIn their poor praise he humbled. Such a man\nMight be a copy to these younger times;\nWhich, follow'd well, would demonstrate them now\nBut goers backward.","sourceLine":"1.2.30","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:1.2:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"His good remembrance, sir,\nLies richer in your thoughts than on his tomb;\nSo in approof lives not his epitaph\nAs in your royal speech.","sourceLine":"1.2.55","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:1.2:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Would I were with him! He would always say--\nMethinks I hear him now; his plausive words\nHe scatter'd not in ears, but grafted them,\nTo grow there and to bear,--'Let me not live,'--\nThis his good melancholy oft began,\nOn the catastrophe and heel of pastime,\nWhen it was out,--'Let me not live,' quoth he,\n'After my flame lacks oil, to be the snuff\nOf younger spirits, whose apprehensive senses\nAll but new things disdain; whose judgments are\nMere fathers of their garments; whose constancies\nExpire before their fashions.' This he wish'd;\nI after him do after him wish too,\nSince I nor wax nor honey can bring home,\nI quickly were dissolved from my hive,\nTo give some labourers room.","sourceLine":"1.2.59","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:1.2:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You are loved, sir:\nThey that least lend it you shall lack you first.","sourceLine":"1.2.75","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:1.2:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I fill a place, I know't. How long is't, count,\nSince the physician at your father's died?\nHe was much famed.","sourceLine":"1.2.77","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:1.2:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Some six months since, my lord.","sourceLine":"1.2.80","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:1.2:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If he were living, I would try him yet.\nLend me an arm; the rest have worn me out\nWith several applications; nature and sickness\nDebate it at their leisure. Welcome, count;\nMy son's no dearer.","sourceLine":"1.2.81","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:1.2:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thank your majesty.","sourceLine":"1.2.86","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:1.2:20"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt. Flourish","id":"allswell:1.2:21"}],"scene":2},{"id":"1.3","label":"Act 1 · SCENE III. Rousillon. The COUNT's palace.","act":1,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/allswell/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter COUNTESS, Steward, and Clown","id":"allswell:1.3:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I will now hear; what say you of this gentlewoman?","sourceLine":"1.3.1","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:1.3:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Madam, the care I have had to even your content, I\nwish might be found in the calendar of my past\nendeavours; for then we wound our modesty and make\nfoul the clearness of our deservings, when of\nourselves we publish them.","sourceLine":"1.3.2","speaker":"Steward","id":"allswell:1.3:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What does this knave here? Get you gone, sirrah:\nthe complaints I have heard of you I do not all\nbelieve: 'tis my slowness that I do not; for I know\nyou lack not folly to commit them, and have ability\nenough to make such knaveries yours.","sourceLine":"1.3.7","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:1.3:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'Tis not unknown to you, madam, I am a poor fellow.","sourceLine":"1.3.12","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:1.3:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well, sir.","sourceLine":"1.3.13","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:1.3:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No, madam, 'tis not so well that I am poor, though\nmany of the rich are damned: but, if I may have\nyour ladyship's good will to go to the world, Isbel\nthe woman and I will do as we may.","sourceLine":"1.3.14","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:1.3:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Wilt thou needs be a beggar?","sourceLine":"1.3.18","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:1.3:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I do beg your good will in this case.","sourceLine":"1.3.19","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:1.3:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"In what case?","sourceLine":"1.3.20","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:1.3:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"In Isbel's case and mine own. Service is no\nheritage: and I think I shall never have the\nblessing of God till I have issue o' my body; for\nthey say barnes are blessings.","sourceLine":"1.3.21","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:1.3:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Tell me thy reason why thou wilt marry.","sourceLine":"1.3.25","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:1.3:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My poor body, madam, requires it: I am driven on\nby the flesh; and he must needs go that the devil drives.","sourceLine":"1.3.26","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:1.3:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Is this all your worship's reason?","sourceLine":"1.3.28","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:1.3:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Faith, madam, I have other holy reasons such as they\nare.","sourceLine":"1.3.29","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:1.3:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"May the world know them?","sourceLine":"1.3.31","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:1.3:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I have been, madam, a wicked creature, as you and\nall flesh and blood are; and, indeed, I do marry\nthat I may repent.","sourceLine":"1.3.32","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:1.3:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thy marriage, sooner than thy wickedness.","sourceLine":"1.3.35","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:1.3:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I am out o' friends, madam; and I hope to have\nfriends for my wife's sake.","sourceLine":"1.3.36","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:1.3:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Such friends are thine enemies, knave.","sourceLine":"1.3.38","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:1.3:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You're shallow, madam, in great friends; for the\nknaves come to do that for me which I am aweary of.\nHe that ears my land spares my team and gives me\nleave to in the crop; if I be his cuckold, he's my\ndrudge: he that comforts my wife is the cherisher\nof my flesh and blood; he that cherishes my flesh\nand blood loves my flesh and blood; he that loves my\nflesh and blood is my friend: ergo, he that kisses\nmy wife is my friend. If men could be contented to\nbe what they are, there were no fear in marriage;\nfor young Charbon the Puritan and old Poysam the\nPapist, howsome'er their hearts are severed in\nreligion, their heads are both one; they may jowl\nhorns together, like any deer i' the herd.","sourceLine":"1.3.39","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:1.3:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Wilt thou ever be a foul-mouthed and calumnious knave?","sourceLine":"1.3.53","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:1.3:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A prophet I, madam; and I speak the truth the next\nway:\nFor I the ballad will repeat,\nWhich men full true shall find;\nYour marriage comes by destiny,\nYour cuckoo sings by kind.","sourceLine":"1.3.54","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:1.3:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Get you gone, sir; I'll talk with you more anon.","sourceLine":"1.3.60","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:1.3:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"May it please you, madam, that he bid Helen come to\nyou: of her I am to speak.","sourceLine":"1.3.61","speaker":"Steward","id":"allswell:1.3:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sirrah, tell my gentlewoman I would speak with her;\nHelen, I mean.","sourceLine":"1.3.63","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:1.3:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Was this fair face the cause, quoth she,\nWhy the Grecians sacked Troy?\nFond done, done fond,\nWas this King Priam's joy?\nWith that she sighed as she stood,\nWith that she sighed as she stood,\nAnd gave this sentence then;\nAmong nine bad if one be good,\nAmong nine bad if one be good,\nThere's yet one good in ten.","sourceLine":"1.3.65","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:1.3:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What, one good in ten? you corrupt the song, sirrah.","sourceLine":"1.3.75","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:1.3:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"One good woman in ten, madam; which is a purifying\no' the song: would God would serve the world so all\nthe year! we'ld find no fault with the tithe-woman,\nif I were the parson. One in ten, quoth a'! An we\nmight have a good woman born but one every blazing\nstar, or at an earthquake, 'twould mend the lottery\nwell: a man may draw his heart out, ere a' pluck\none.","sourceLine":"1.3.76","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:1.3:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You'll be gone, sir knave, and do as I command you.","sourceLine":"1.3.84","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:1.3:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That man should be at woman's command, and yet no\nhurt done! Though honesty be no puritan, yet it\nwill do no hurt; it will wear the surplice of\nhumility over the black gown of a big heart. I am\ngoing, forsooth: the business is for Helen to come hither.","sourceLine":"1.3.85","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:1.3:31"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"allswell:1.3:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well, now.","sourceLine":"1.3.90","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:1.3:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I know, madam, you love your gentlewoman entirely.","sourceLine":"1.3.91","speaker":"Steward","id":"allswell:1.3:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Faith, I do: her father bequeathed her to me; and\nshe herself, without other advantage, may lawfully\nmake title to as much love as she finds: there is\nmore owing her than is paid; and more shall be paid\nher than she'll demand.","sourceLine":"1.3.92","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:1.3:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Madam, I was very late more near her than I think\nshe wished me: alone she was, and did communicate\nto herself her own words to her own ears; she\nthought, I dare vow for her, they touched not any\nstranger sense. Her matter was, she loved your son:\nFortune, she said, was no goddess, that had put\nsuch difference betwixt their two estates; Love no\ngod, that would not extend his might, only where\nqualities were level; Dian no queen of virgins, that\nwould suffer her poor knight surprised, without\nrescue in the first assault or ransom afterward.\nThis she delivered in the most bitter touch of\nsorrow that e'er I heard virgin exclaim in: which I\nheld my duty speedily to acquaint you withal;\nsithence, in the loss that may happen, it concerns\nyou something to know it.","sourceLine":"1.3.97","speaker":"Steward","id":"allswell:1.3:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You have discharged this honestly; keep it to\nyourself: many likelihoods informed me of this\nbefore, which hung so tottering in the balance that\nI could neither believe nor misdoubt. Pray you,\nleave me: stall this in your bosom; and I thank you\nfor your honest care: I will speak with you further anon.","sourceLine":"1.3.113","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:1.3:37"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit Steward","id":"allswell:1.3:38"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter HELENA","id":"allswell:1.3:39"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Even so it was with me when I was young:\nIf ever we are nature's, these are ours; this thorn\nDoth to our rose of youth rightly belong;\nOur blood to us, this to our blood is born;\nIt is the show and seal of nature's truth,\nWhere love's strong passion is impress'd in youth:\nBy our remembrances of days foregone,\nSuch were our faults, or then we thought them none.\nHer eye is sick on't: I observe her now.","sourceLine":"1.3.119","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:1.3:40"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What is your pleasure, madam?","sourceLine":"1.3.128","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:1.3:41"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You know, Helen,\nI am a mother to you.","sourceLine":"1.3.129","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:1.3:42"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Mine honourable mistress.","sourceLine":"1.3.131","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:1.3:43"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, a mother:\nWhy not a mother? When I said 'a mother,'\nMethought you saw a serpent: what's in 'mother,'\nThat you start at it? I say, I am your mother;\nAnd put you in the catalogue of those\nThat were enwombed mine: 'tis often seen\nAdoption strives with nature and choice breeds\nA native slip to us from foreign seeds:\nYou ne'er oppress'd me with a mother's groan,\nYet I express to you a mother's care:\nGod's mercy, maiden! does it curd thy blood\nTo say I am thy mother? What's the matter,\nThat this distemper'd messenger of wet,\nThe many-colour'd Iris, rounds thine eye?\nWhy? that you are my daughter?","sourceLine":"1.3.132","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:1.3:44"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That I am not.","sourceLine":"1.3.147","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:1.3:45"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I say, I am your mother.","sourceLine":"1.3.148","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:1.3:46"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Pardon, madam;\nThe Count Rousillon cannot be my brother:\nI am from humble, he from honour'd name;\nNo note upon my parents, his all noble:\nMy master, my dear lord he is; and I\nHis servant live, and will his vassal die:\nHe must not be my brother.","sourceLine":"1.3.149","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:1.3:47"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nor I your mother?","sourceLine":"1.3.156","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:1.3:48"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You are my mother, madam; would you were,--\nSo that my lord your son were not my brother,--\nIndeed my mother! or were you both our mothers,\nI care no more for than I do for heaven,\nSo I were not his sister. Can't no other,\nBut, I your daughter, he must be my brother?","sourceLine":"1.3.157","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:1.3:49"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes, Helen, you might be my daughter-in-law:\nGod shield you mean it not! daughter and mother\nSo strive upon your pulse. What, pale again?\nMy fear hath catch'd your fondness: now I see\nThe mystery of your loneliness, and find\nYour salt tears' head: now to all sense 'tis gross\nYou love my son; invention is ashamed,\nAgainst the proclamation of thy passion,\nTo say thou dost not: therefore tell me true;\nBut tell me then, 'tis so; for, look thy cheeks\nConfess it, th' one to th' other; and thine eyes\nSee it so grossly shown in thy behaviors\nThat in their kind they speak it: only sin\nAnd hellish obstinacy tie thy tongue,\nThat truth should be suspected. Speak, is't so?\nIf it be so, you have wound a goodly clew;\nIf it be not, forswear't: howe'er, I charge thee,\nAs heaven shall work in me for thine avail,\nTell me truly.","sourceLine":"1.3.163","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:1.3:50"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Good madam, pardon me!","sourceLine":"1.3.182","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:1.3:51"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Do you love my son?","sourceLine":"1.3.183","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:1.3:52"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Your pardon, noble mistress!","sourceLine":"1.3.184","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:1.3:53"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Love you my son?","sourceLine":"1.3.185","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:1.3:54"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Do not you love him, madam?","sourceLine":"1.3.186","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:1.3:55"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Go not about; my love hath in't a bond,\nWhereof the world takes note: come, come, disclose\nThe state of your affection; for your passions\nHave to the full appeach'd.","sourceLine":"1.3.187","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:1.3:56"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Then, I confess,\nHere on my knee, before high heaven and you,\nThat before you, and next unto high heaven,\nI love your son.\nMy friends were poor, but honest; so's my love:\nBe not offended; for it hurts not him\nThat he is loved of me: I follow him not\nBy any token of presumptuous suit;\nNor would I have him till I do deserve him;\nYet never know how that desert should be.\nI know I love in vain, strive against hope;\nYet in this captious and intenible sieve\nI still pour in the waters of my love\nAnd lack not to lose still: thus, Indian-like,\nReligious in mine error, I adore\nThe sun, that looks upon his worshipper,\nBut knows of him no more. My dearest madam,\nLet not your hate encounter with my love\nFor loving where you do: but if yourself,\nWhose aged honour cites a virtuous youth,\nDid ever in so true a flame of liking\nWish chastely and love dearly, that your Dian\nWas both herself and love: O, then, give pity\nTo her, whose state is such that cannot choose\nBut lend and give where she is sure to lose;\nThat seeks not to find that her search implies,\nBut riddle-like lives sweetly where she dies!","sourceLine":"1.3.191","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:1.3:57"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Had you not lately an intent,--speak truly,--\nTo go to Paris?","sourceLine":"1.3.218","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:1.3:58"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Madam, I had.","sourceLine":"1.3.220","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:1.3:59"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Wherefore? tell true.","sourceLine":"1.3.221","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:1.3:60"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I will tell truth; by grace itself I swear.\nYou know my father left me some prescriptions\nOf rare and proved effects, such as his reading\nAnd manifest experience had collected\nFor general sovereignty; and that he will'd me\nIn heedfull'st reservation to bestow them,\nAs notes whose faculties inclusive were\nMore than they were in note: amongst the rest,\nThere is a remedy, approved, set down,\nTo cure the desperate languishings whereof\nThe king is render'd lost.","sourceLine":"1.3.222","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:1.3:61"},{"kind":"speech","text":"This was your motive\nFor Paris, was it? speak.","sourceLine":"1.3.233","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:1.3:62"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord your son made me to think of this;\nElse Paris and the medicine and the king\nHad from the conversation of my thoughts\nHaply been absent then.","sourceLine":"1.3.235","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:1.3:63"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But think you, Helen,\nIf you should tender your supposed aid,\nHe would receive it? he and his physicians\nAre of a mind; he, that they cannot help him,\nThey, that they cannot help: how shall they credit\nA poor unlearned virgin, when the schools,\nEmbowell'd of their doctrine, have left off\nThe danger to itself?","sourceLine":"1.3.239","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:1.3:64"},{"kind":"speech","text":"There's something in't,\nMore than my father's skill, which was the greatest\nOf his profession, that his good receipt\nShall for my legacy be sanctified\nBy the luckiest stars in heaven: and, would your honour\nBut give me leave to try success, I'ld venture\nThe well-lost life of mine on his grace's cure\nBy such a day and hour.","sourceLine":"1.3.247","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:1.3:65"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Dost thou believe't?","sourceLine":"1.3.255","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:1.3:66"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, madam, knowingly.","sourceLine":"1.3.256","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:1.3:67"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, Helen, thou shalt have my leave and love,\nMeans and attendants and my loving greetings\nTo those of mine in court: I'll stay at home\nAnd pray God's blessing into thy attempt:\nBe gone to-morrow; and be sure of this,\nWhat I can help thee to thou shalt not miss.","sourceLine":"1.3.257","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:1.3:68"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"allswell:1.3:69"}],"scene":3},{"id":"2.1","label":"Act 2 · SCENE I. Paris. The KING's palace.","act":2,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/allswell/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Flourish of cornets. Enter the KING, attended with divers young Lords taking leave for the Florentine war; BERTRAM, and PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.1:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Farewell, young lords; these warlike principles\nDo not throw from you: and you, my lords, farewell:\nShare the advice betwixt you; if both gain, all\nThe gift doth stretch itself as 'tis received,\nAnd is enough for both.","sourceLine":"2.1.1","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:2.1:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'Tis our hope, sir,\nAfter well enter'd soldiers, to return\nAnd find your grace in health.","sourceLine":"2.1.6","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:2.1:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No, no, it cannot be; and yet my heart\nWill not confess he owes the malady\nThat doth my life besiege. Farewell, young lords;\nWhether I live or die, be you the sons\nOf worthy Frenchmen: let higher Italy,--\nThose bated that inherit but the fall\nOf the last monarchy,--see that you come\nNot to woo honour, but to wed it; when\nThe bravest questant shrinks, find what you seek,\nThat fame may cry you loud: I say, farewell.","sourceLine":"2.1.9","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:2.1:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Health, at your bidding, serve your majesty!","sourceLine":"2.1.19","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:2.1:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Those girls of Italy, take heed of them:\nThey say, our French lack language to deny,\nIf they demand: beware of being captives,\nBefore you serve.","sourceLine":"2.1.20","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:2.1:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Our hearts receive your warnings.","sourceLine":"2.1.24","speaker":"Both","id":"allswell:2.1:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Farewell. Come hither to me.","sourceLine":"2.1.25","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:2.1:8"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit, attended","id":"allswell:2.1:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, my sweet lord, that you will stay behind us!","sourceLine":"2.1.26","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:2.1:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'Tis not his fault, the spark.","sourceLine":"2.1.27","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.1:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, 'tis brave wars!","sourceLine":"2.1.28","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:2.1:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Most admirable: I have seen those wars.","sourceLine":"2.1.29","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.1:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I am commanded here, and kept a coil with\n'Too young' and 'the next year' and ''tis too early.'","sourceLine":"2.1.30","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:2.1:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"An thy mind stand to't, boy, steal away bravely.","sourceLine":"2.1.32","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.1:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I shall stay here the forehorse to a smock,\nCreaking my shoes on the plain masonry,\nTill honour be bought up and no sword worn\nBut one to dance with! By heaven, I'll steal away.","sourceLine":"2.1.33","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:2.1:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"There's honour in the theft.","sourceLine":"2.1.37","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:2.1:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Commit it, count.","sourceLine":"2.1.38","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.1:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I am your accessary; and so, farewell.","sourceLine":"2.1.39","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:2.1:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I grow to you, and our parting is a tortured body.","sourceLine":"2.1.40","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:2.1:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Farewell, captain.","sourceLine":"2.1.41","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:2.1:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sweet Monsieur Parolles!","sourceLine":"2.1.42","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:2.1:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Noble heroes, my sword and yours are kin. Good\nsparks and lustrous, a word, good metals: you shall\nfind in the regiment of the Spinii one Captain\nSpurio, with his cicatrice, an emblem of war, here\non his sinister cheek; it was this very sword\nentrenched it: say to him, I live; and observe his\nreports for me.","sourceLine":"2.1.43","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.1:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"We shall, noble captain.","sourceLine":"2.1.50","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:2.1:24"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt Lords","id":"allswell:2.1:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Mars dote on you for his novices! what will ye do?","sourceLine":"2.1.51","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.1:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Stay: the king.","sourceLine":"2.1.52","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:2.1:27"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Re-enter KING. BERTRAM and PAROLLES retire","id":"allswell:2.1:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[To BERTRAM] Use a more spacious ceremony to the\nnoble lords; you have restrained yourself within the\nlist of too cold an adieu: be more expressive to\nthem: for they wear themselves in the cap of the\ntime, there do muster true gait, eat, speak, and\nmove under the influence of the most received star;\nand though the devil lead the measure, such are to\nbe followed: after them, and take a more dilated farewell.","sourceLine":"2.1.53","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.1:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And I will do so.","sourceLine":"2.1.61","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:2.1:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Worthy fellows; and like to prove most sinewy sword-men.","sourceLine":"2.1.62","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.1:31"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt BERTRAM and PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.1:32"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.1:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Kneeling] Pardon, my lord, for me and for my tidings.","sourceLine":"2.1.63","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.1:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I'll fee thee to stand up.","sourceLine":"2.1.64","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:2.1:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Then here's a man stands, that has brought his pardon.\nI would you had kneel'd, my lord, to ask me mercy,\nAnd that at my bidding you could so stand up.","sourceLine":"2.1.65","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.1:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I would I had; so I had broke thy pate,\nAnd ask'd thee mercy for't.","sourceLine":"2.1.68","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:2.1:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Good faith, across: but, my good lord 'tis thus;\nWill you be cured of your infirmity?","sourceLine":"2.1.70","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.1:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No.","sourceLine":"2.1.72","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:2.1:39"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, will you eat no grapes, my royal fox?\nYes, but you will my noble grapes, an if\nMy royal fox could reach them: I have seen a medicine\nThat's able to breathe life into a stone,\nQuicken a rock, and make you dance canary\nWith spritely fire and motion; whose simple touch,\nIs powerful to araise King Pepin, nay,\nTo give great Charlemain a pen in's hand,\nAnd write to her a love-line.","sourceLine":"2.1.73","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.1:40"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What 'her' is this?","sourceLine":"2.1.82","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:2.1:41"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, Doctor She: my lord, there's one arrived,\nIf you will see her: now, by my faith and honour,\nIf seriously I may convey my thoughts\nIn this my light deliverance, I have spoke\nWith one that, in her sex, her years, profession,\nWisdom and constancy, hath amazed me more\nThan I dare blame my weakness: will you see her\nFor that is her demand, and know her business?\nThat done, laugh well at me.","sourceLine":"2.1.83","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.1:42"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now, good Lafeu,\nBring in the admiration; that we with thee\nMay spend our wonder too, or take off thine\nBy wondering how thou took'st it.","sourceLine":"2.1.92","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:2.1:43"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, I'll fit you,\nAnd not be all day neither.","sourceLine":"2.1.96","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.1:44"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"allswell:2.1:45"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thus he his special nothing ever prologues.","sourceLine":"2.1.98","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:2.1:46"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Re-enter LAFEU, with HELENA","id":"allswell:2.1:47"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, come your ways.","sourceLine":"2.1.99","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.1:48"},{"kind":"speech","text":"This haste hath wings indeed.","sourceLine":"2.1.100","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:2.1:49"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, come your ways:\nThis is his majesty; say your mind to him:\nA traitor you do look like; but such traitors\nHis majesty seldom fears: I am Cressid's uncle,\nThat dare leave two together; fare you well.","sourceLine":"2.1.101","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.1:50"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"allswell:2.1:51"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now, fair one, does your business follow us?","sourceLine":"2.1.106","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:2.1:52"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, my good lord.\nGerard de Narbon was my father;\nIn what he did profess, well found.","sourceLine":"2.1.107","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:2.1:53"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I knew him.","sourceLine":"2.1.110","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:2.1:54"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The rather will I spare my praises towards him:\nKnowing him is enough. On's bed of death\nMany receipts he gave me: chiefly one.\nWhich, as the dearest issue of his practise,\nAnd of his old experience the oily darling,\nHe bade me store up, as a triple eye,\nSafer than mine own two, more dear; I have so;\nAnd hearing your high majesty is touch'd\nWith that malignant cause wherein the honour\nOf my dear father's gift stands chief in power,\nI come to tender it and my appliance\nWith all bound humbleness.","sourceLine":"2.1.111","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:2.1:55"},{"kind":"speech","text":"We thank you, maiden;\nBut may not be so credulous of cure,\nWhen our most learned doctors leave us and\nThe congregated college have concluded\nThat labouring art can never ransom nature\nFrom her inaidible estate; I say we must not\nSo stain our judgment, or corrupt our hope,\nTo prostitute our past-cure malady\nTo empirics, or to dissever so\nOur great self and our credit, to esteem\nA senseless help when help past sense we deem.","sourceLine":"2.1.123","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:2.1:56"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My duty then shall pay me for my pains:\nI will no more enforce mine office on you.\nHumbly entreating from your royal thoughts\nA modest one, to bear me back again.","sourceLine":"2.1.134","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:2.1:57"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I cannot give thee less, to be call'd grateful:\nThou thought'st to help me; and such thanks I give\nAs one near death to those that wish him live:\nBut what at full I know, thou know'st no part,\nI knowing all my peril, thou no art.","sourceLine":"2.1.138","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:2.1:58"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What I can do can do no hurt to try,\nSince you set up your rest 'gainst remedy.\nHe that of greatest works is finisher\nOft does them by the weakest minister:\nSo holy writ in babes hath judgment shown,\nWhen judges have been babes; great floods have flown\nFrom simple sources, and great seas have dried\nWhen miracles have by the greatest been denied.\nOft expectation fails and most oft there\nWhere most it promises, and oft it hits\nWhere hope is coldest and despair most fits.","sourceLine":"2.1.143","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:2.1:59"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I must not hear thee; fare thee well, kind maid;\nThy pains not used must by thyself be paid:\nProffers not took reap thanks for their reward.","sourceLine":"2.1.154","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:2.1:60"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Inspired merit so by breath is barr'd:\nIt is not so with Him that all things knows\nAs 'tis with us that square our guess by shows;\nBut most it is presumption in us when\nThe help of heaven we count the act of men.\nDear sir, to my endeavours give consent;\nOf heaven, not me, make an experiment.\nI am not an impostor that proclaim\nMyself against the level of mine aim;\nBut know I think and think I know most sure\nMy art is not past power nor you past cure.","sourceLine":"2.1.157","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:2.1:61"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Are thou so confident? within what space\nHopest thou my cure?","sourceLine":"2.1.168","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:2.1:62"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The great'st grace lending grace\nEre twice the horses of the sun shall bring\nTheir fiery torcher his diurnal ring,\nEre twice in murk and occidental damp\nMoist Hesperus hath quench'd his sleepy lamp,\nOr four and twenty times the pilot's glass\nHath told the thievish minutes how they pass,\nWhat is infirm from your sound parts shall fly,\nHealth shall live free and sickness freely die.","sourceLine":"2.1.170","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:2.1:63"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Upon thy certainty and confidence\nWhat darest thou venture?","sourceLine":"2.1.179","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:2.1:64"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Tax of impudence,\nA strumpet's boldness, a divulged shame\nTraduced by odious ballads: my maiden's name\nSear'd otherwise; nay, worse--if worse--extended\nWith vilest torture let my life be ended.","sourceLine":"2.1.181","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:2.1:65"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Methinks in thee some blessed spirit doth speak\nHis powerful sound within an organ weak:\nAnd what impossibility would slay\nIn common sense, sense saves another way.\nThy life is dear; for all that life can rate\nWorth name of life in thee hath estimate,\nYouth, beauty, wisdom, courage, all\nThat happiness and prime can happy call:\nThou this to hazard needs must intimate\nSkill infinite or monstrous desperate.\nSweet practiser, thy physic I will try,\nThat ministers thine own death if I die.","sourceLine":"2.1.186","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:2.1:66"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If I break time, or flinch in property\nOf what I spoke, unpitied let me die,\nAnd well deserved: not helping, death's my fee;\nBut, if I help, what do you promise me?","sourceLine":"2.1.198","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:2.1:67"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Make thy demand.","sourceLine":"2.1.202","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:2.1:68"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But will you make it even?","sourceLine":"2.1.203","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:2.1:69"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, by my sceptre and my hopes of heaven.","sourceLine":"2.1.204","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:2.1:70"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Then shalt thou give me with thy kingly hand\nWhat husband in thy power I will command:\nExempted be from me the arrogance\nTo choose from forth the royal blood of France,\nMy low and humble name to propagate\nWith any branch or image of thy state;\nBut such a one, thy vassal, whom I know\nIs free for me to ask, thee to bestow.","sourceLine":"2.1.205","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:2.1:71"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Here is my hand; the premises observed,\nThy will by my performance shall be served:\nSo make the choice of thy own time, for I,\nThy resolved patient, on thee still rely.\nMore should I question thee, and more I must,\nThough more to know could not be more to trust,\nFrom whence thou camest, how tended on: but rest\nUnquestion'd welcome and undoubted blest.\nGive me some help here, ho! If thou proceed\nAs high as word, my deed shall match thy meed.","sourceLine":"2.1.213","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:2.1:72"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Flourish. Exeunt","id":"allswell:2.1:73"}],"scene":1},{"id":"2.2","label":"Act 2 · SCENE II. Rousillon. The COUNT's palace.","act":2,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/allswell/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter COUNTESS and Clown","id":"allswell:2.2:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come on, sir; I shall now put you to the height of\nyour breeding.","sourceLine":"2.2.1","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:2.2:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I will show myself highly fed and lowly taught: I\nknow my business is but to the court.","sourceLine":"2.2.3","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:2.2:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"To the court! why, what place make you special,\nwhen you put off that with such contempt? But to the court!","sourceLine":"2.2.5","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:2.2:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Truly, madam, if God have lent a man any manners, he\nmay easily put it off at court: he that cannot make\na leg, put off's cap, kiss his hand and say nothing,\nhas neither leg, hands, lip, nor cap; and indeed\nsuch a fellow, to say precisely, were not for the\ncourt; but for me, I have an answer will serve all\nmen.","sourceLine":"2.2.7","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:2.2:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Marry, that's a bountiful answer that fits all\nquestions.","sourceLine":"2.2.14","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:2.2:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"It is like a barber's chair that fits all buttocks,\nthe pin-buttock, the quatch-buttock, the brawn\nbuttock, or any buttock.","sourceLine":"2.2.16","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:2.2:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Will your answer serve fit to all questions?","sourceLine":"2.2.19","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:2.2:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"As fit as ten groats is for the hand of an attorney,\nas your French crown for your taffeta punk, as Tib's\nrush for Tom's forefinger, as a pancake for Shrove\nTuesday, a morris for May-day, as the nail to his\nhole, the cuckold to his horn, as a scolding queen\nto a wrangling knave, as the nun's lip to the\nfriar's mouth, nay, as the pudding to his skin.","sourceLine":"2.2.20","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:2.2:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Have you, I say, an answer of such fitness for all\nquestions?","sourceLine":"2.2.27","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:2.2:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"From below your duke to beneath your constable, it\nwill fit any question.","sourceLine":"2.2.29","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:2.2:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"It must be an answer of most monstrous size that\nmust fit all demands.","sourceLine":"2.2.31","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:2.2:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But a trifle neither, in good faith, if the learned\nshould speak truth of it: here it is, and all that\nbelongs to't. Ask me if I am a courtier: it shall\ndo you no harm to learn.","sourceLine":"2.2.33","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:2.2:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"To be young again, if we could: I will be a fool in\nquestion, hoping to be the wiser by your answer. I\npray you, sir, are you a courtier?","sourceLine":"2.2.37","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:2.2:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O Lord, sir! There's a simple putting off. More,\nmore, a hundred of them.","sourceLine":"2.2.40","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:2.2:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sir, I am a poor friend of yours, that loves you.","sourceLine":"2.2.42","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:2.2:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O Lord, sir! Thick, thick, spare not me.","sourceLine":"2.2.43","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:2.2:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I think, sir, you can eat none of this homely meat.","sourceLine":"2.2.44","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:2.2:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O Lord, sir! Nay, put me to't, I warrant you.","sourceLine":"2.2.45","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:2.2:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You were lately whipped, sir, as I think.","sourceLine":"2.2.46","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:2.2:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O Lord, sir! spare not me.","sourceLine":"2.2.47","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:2.2:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Do you cry, 'O Lord, sir!' at your whipping, and\n'spare not me?' Indeed your 'O Lord, sir!' is very\nsequent to your whipping: you would answer very well\nto a whipping, if you were but bound to't.","sourceLine":"2.2.48","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:2.2:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I ne'er had worse luck in my life in my 'O Lord,\nsir!' I see things may serve long, but not serve ever.","sourceLine":"2.2.52","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:2.2:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I play the noble housewife with the time\nTo entertain't so merrily with a fool.","sourceLine":"2.2.54","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:2.2:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O Lord, sir! why, there't serves well again.","sourceLine":"2.2.56","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:2.2:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"An end, sir; to your business. Give Helen this,\nAnd urge her to a present answer back:\nCommend me to my kinsmen and my son:\nThis is not much.","sourceLine":"2.2.57","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:2.2:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Not much commendation to them.","sourceLine":"2.2.61","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:2.2:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Not much employment for you: you understand me?","sourceLine":"2.2.62","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:2.2:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Most fruitfully: I am there before my legs.","sourceLine":"2.2.63","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:2.2:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Haste you again.","sourceLine":"2.2.64","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:2.2:30"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt severally","id":"allswell:2.2:31"}],"scene":2},{"id":"2.3","label":"Act 2 · SCENE III. Paris. The KING's palace.","act":2,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/allswell/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter BERTRAM, LAFEU, and PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.3:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"They say miracles are past; and we have our\nphilosophical persons, to make modern and familiar,\nthings supernatural and causeless. Hence is it that\nwe make trifles of terrors, ensconcing ourselves\ninto seeming knowledge, when we should submit\nourselves to an unknown fear.","sourceLine":"2.3.1","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.3:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, 'tis the rarest argument of wonder that hath\nshot out in our latter times.","sourceLine":"2.3.7","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.3:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And so 'tis.","sourceLine":"2.3.9","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:2.3:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"To be relinquish'd of the artists,--","sourceLine":"2.3.10","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.3:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So I say.","sourceLine":"2.3.11","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.3:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Both of Galen and Paracelsus.","sourceLine":"2.3.12","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.3:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So I say.","sourceLine":"2.3.13","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.3:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Of all the learned and authentic fellows,--","sourceLine":"2.3.14","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.3:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Right; so I say.","sourceLine":"2.3.15","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.3:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That gave him out incurable,--","sourceLine":"2.3.16","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.3:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, there 'tis; so say I too.","sourceLine":"2.3.17","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.3:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Not to be helped,--","sourceLine":"2.3.18","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.3:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Right; as 'twere, a man assured of a--","sourceLine":"2.3.19","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.3:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Uncertain life, and sure death.","sourceLine":"2.3.20","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.3:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Just, you say well; so would I have said.","sourceLine":"2.3.21","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.3:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I may truly say, it is a novelty to the world.","sourceLine":"2.3.22","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.3:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"It is, indeed: if you will have it in showing, you\nshall read it in--what do you call there?","sourceLine":"2.3.23","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.3:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A showing of a heavenly effect in an earthly actor.","sourceLine":"2.3.25","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.3:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That's it; I would have said the very same.","sourceLine":"2.3.26","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.3:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, your dolphin is not lustier: 'fore me,\nI speak in respect--","sourceLine":"2.3.27","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.3:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, 'tis strange, 'tis very strange, that is the\nbrief and the tedious of it; and he's of a most\nfacinerious spirit that will not acknowledge it to be the--","sourceLine":"2.3.29","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.3:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Very hand of heaven.","sourceLine":"2.3.32","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.3:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, so I say.","sourceLine":"2.3.33","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.3:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"In a most weak--","sourceLine":"2.3.34","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.3:25"},{"kind":"stage","text":"pausing","id":"allswell:2.3:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"and debile minister, great power, great\ntranscendence: which should, indeed, give us a\nfurther use to be made than alone the recovery of\nthe king, as to be--","sourceLine":"2.3.35","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.3:27"},{"kind":"stage","text":"pausing","id":"allswell:2.3:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"generally thankful.","sourceLine":"2.3.39","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.3:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I would have said it; you say well. Here comes the king.","sourceLine":"2.3.40","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.3:30"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter KING, HELENA, and Attendants. LAFEU and PAROLLES retire","id":"allswell:2.3:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Lustig, as the Dutchman says: I'll like a maid the\nbetter, whilst I have a tooth in my head: why, he's\nable to lead her a coranto.","sourceLine":"2.3.41","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.3:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Mort du vinaigre! is not this Helen?","sourceLine":"2.3.44","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.3:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'Fore God, I think so.","sourceLine":"2.3.45","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.3:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Go, call before me all the lords in court.\nSit, my preserver, by thy patient's side;\nAnd with this healthful hand, whose banish'd sense\nThou hast repeal'd, a second time receive\nThe confirmation of my promised gift,\nWhich but attends thy naming.","sourceLine":"2.3.46","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:2.3:35"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter three or four Lords","id":"allswell:2.3:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Fair maid, send forth thine eye: this youthful parcel\nOf noble bachelors stand at my bestowing,\nO'er whom both sovereign power and father's voice\nI have to use: thy frank election make;\nThou hast power to choose, and they none to forsake.","sourceLine":"2.3.52","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:2.3:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"To each of you one fair and virtuous mistress\nFall, when Love please! marry, to each, but one!","sourceLine":"2.3.57","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:2.3:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I'ld give bay Curtal and his furniture,\nMy mouth no more were broken than these boys',\nAnd writ as little beard.","sourceLine":"2.3.59","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.3:39"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Peruse them well:\nNot one of those but had a noble father.","sourceLine":"2.3.62","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:2.3:40"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Gentlemen,\nHeaven hath through me restored the king to health.","sourceLine":"2.3.64","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:2.3:41"},{"kind":"speech","text":"We understand it, and thank heaven for you.","sourceLine":"2.3.66","speaker":"All","id":"allswell:2.3:42"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I am a simple maid, and therein wealthiest,\nThat I protest I simply am a maid.\nPlease it your majesty, I have done already:\nThe blushes in my cheeks thus whisper me,\n'We blush that thou shouldst choose; but, be refused,\nLet the white death sit on thy cheek for ever;\nWe'll ne'er come there again.'","sourceLine":"2.3.67","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:2.3:43"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Make choice; and, see,\nWho shuns thy love shuns all his love in me.","sourceLine":"2.3.74","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:2.3:44"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now, Dian, from thy altar do I fly,\nAnd to imperial Love, that god most high,\nDo my sighs stream. Sir, will you hear my suit?","sourceLine":"2.3.76","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:2.3:45"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And grant it.","sourceLine":"2.3.79","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:2.3:46"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thanks, sir; all the rest is mute.","sourceLine":"2.3.80","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:2.3:47"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I had rather be in this choice than throw ames-ace\nfor my life.","sourceLine":"2.3.81","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.3:48"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The honour, sir, that flames in your fair eyes,\nBefore I speak, too threateningly replies:\nLove make your fortunes twenty times above\nHer that so wishes and her humble love!","sourceLine":"2.3.83","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:2.3:49"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No better, if you please.","sourceLine":"2.3.87","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:2.3:50"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My wish receive,\nWhich great Love grant! and so, I take my leave.","sourceLine":"2.3.88","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:2.3:51"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Do all they deny her? An they were sons of mine,\nI'd have them whipped; or I would send them to the\nTurk, to make eunuchs of.","sourceLine":"2.3.90","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.3:52"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Be not afraid that I your hand should take;\nI'll never do you wrong for your own sake:\nBlessing upon your vows! and in your bed\nFind fairer fortune, if you ever wed!","sourceLine":"2.3.93","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:2.3:53"},{"kind":"speech","text":"These boys are boys of ice, they'll none have her:\nsure, they are bastards to the English; the French\nne'er got 'em.","sourceLine":"2.3.97","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.3:54"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You are too young, too happy, and too good,\nTo make yourself a son out of my blood.","sourceLine":"2.3.100","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:2.3:55"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Fair one, I think not so.","sourceLine":"2.3.102","speaker":"Fourth Lord","id":"allswell:2.3:56"},{"kind":"speech","text":"There's one grape yet; I am sure thy father drunk\nwine: but if thou be'st not an ass, I am a youth\nof fourteen; I have known thee already.","sourceLine":"2.3.103","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.3:57"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[To BERTRAM] I dare not say I take you; but I give\nMe and my service, ever whilst I live,\nInto your guiding power. This is the man.","sourceLine":"2.3.106","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:2.3:58"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, then, young Bertram, take her; she's thy wife.","sourceLine":"2.3.109","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:2.3:59"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My wife, my liege! I shall beseech your highness,\nIn such a business give me leave to use\nThe help of mine own eyes.","sourceLine":"2.3.110","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:2.3:60"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Know'st thou not, Bertram,\nWhat she has done for me?","sourceLine":"2.3.113","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:2.3:61"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes, my good lord;\nBut never hope to know why I should marry her.","sourceLine":"2.3.115","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:2.3:62"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thou know'st she has raised me from my sickly bed.","sourceLine":"2.3.117","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:2.3:63"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But follows it, my lord, to bring me down\nMust answer for your raising? I know her well:\nShe had her breeding at my father's charge.\nA poor physician's daughter my wife! Disdain\nRather corrupt me ever!","sourceLine":"2.3.118","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:2.3:64"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'Tis only title thou disdain'st in her, the which\nI can build up. Strange is it that our bloods,\nOf colour, weight, and heat, pour'd all together,\nWould quite confound distinction, yet stand off\nIn differences so mighty. If she be\nAll that is virtuous, save what thou dislikest,\nA poor physician's daughter, thou dislikest\nOf virtue for the name: but do not so:\nFrom lowest place when virtuous things proceed,\nThe place is dignified by the doer's deed:\nWhere great additions swell's, and virtue none,\nIt is a dropsied honour. Good alone\nIs good without a name. Vileness is so:\nThe property by what it is should go,\nNot by the title. She is young, wise, fair;\nIn these to nature she's immediate heir,\nAnd these breed honour: that is honour's scorn,\nWhich challenges itself as honour's born\nAnd is not like the sire: honours thrive,\nWhen rather from our acts we them derive\nThan our foregoers: the mere word's a slave\nDebosh'd on every tomb, on every grave\nA lying trophy, and as oft is dumb\nWhere dust and damn'd oblivion is the tomb\nOf honour'd bones indeed. What should be said?\nIf thou canst like this creature as a maid,\nI can create the rest: virtue and she\nIs her own dower; honour and wealth from me.","sourceLine":"2.3.123","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:2.3:65"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I cannot love her, nor will strive to do't.","sourceLine":"2.3.151","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:2.3:66"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thou wrong'st thyself, if thou shouldst strive to choose.","sourceLine":"2.3.152","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:2.3:67"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That you are well restored, my lord, I'm glad:\nLet the rest go.","sourceLine":"2.3.153","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:2.3:68"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My honour's at the stake; which to defeat,\nI must produce my power. Here, take her hand,\nProud scornful boy, unworthy this good gift;\nThat dost in vile misprision shackle up\nMy love and her desert; that canst not dream,\nWe, poising us in her defective scale,\nShall weigh thee to the beam; that wilt not know,\nIt is in us to plant thine honour where\nWe please to have it grow. Cheque thy contempt:\nObey our will, which travails in thy good:\nBelieve not thy disdain, but presently\nDo thine own fortunes that obedient right\nWhich both thy duty owes and our power claims;\nOr I will throw thee from my care for ever\nInto the staggers and the careless lapse\nOf youth and ignorance; both my revenge and hate\nLoosing upon thee, in the name of justice,\nWithout all terms of pity. Speak; thine answer.","sourceLine":"2.3.155","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:2.3:69"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Pardon, my gracious lord; for I submit\nMy fancy to your eyes: when I consider\nWhat great creation and what dole of honour\nFlies where you bid it, I find that she, which late\nWas in my nobler thoughts most base, is now\nThe praised of the king; who, so ennobled,\nIs as 'twere born so.","sourceLine":"2.3.173","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:2.3:70"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Take her by the hand,\nAnd tell her she is thine: to whom I promise\nA counterpoise, if not to thy estate\nA balance more replete.","sourceLine":"2.3.180","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:2.3:71"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I take her hand.","sourceLine":"2.3.184","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:2.3:72"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Good fortune and the favour of the king\nSmile upon this contract; whose ceremony\nShall seem expedient on the now-born brief,\nAnd be perform'd to-night: the solemn feast\nShall more attend upon the coming space,\nExpecting absent friends. As thou lovest her,\nThy love's to me religious; else, does err.","sourceLine":"2.3.185","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:2.3:73"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt all but LAFEU and PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.3:74"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Advancing] Do you hear, monsieur? a word with you.","sourceLine":"2.3.192","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.3:75"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Your pleasure, sir?","sourceLine":"2.3.193","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.3:76"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Your lord and master did well to make his\nrecantation.","sourceLine":"2.3.194","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.3:77"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Recantation! My lord! my master!","sourceLine":"2.3.196","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.3:78"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay; is it not a language I speak?","sourceLine":"2.3.197","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.3:79"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A most harsh one, and not to be understood without\nbloody succeeding. My master!","sourceLine":"2.3.198","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.3:80"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Are you companion to the Count Rousillon?","sourceLine":"2.3.200","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.3:81"},{"kind":"speech","text":"To any count, to all counts, to what is man.","sourceLine":"2.3.201","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.3:82"},{"kind":"speech","text":"To what is count's man: count's master is of\nanother style.","sourceLine":"2.3.202","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.3:83"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You are too old, sir; let it satisfy you, you are too old.","sourceLine":"2.3.204","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.3:84"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I must tell thee, sirrah, I write man; to which\ntitle age cannot bring thee.","sourceLine":"2.3.205","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.3:85"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What I dare too well do, I dare not do.","sourceLine":"2.3.207","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.3:86"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I did think thee, for two ordinaries, to be a pretty\nwise fellow; thou didst make tolerable vent of thy\ntravel; it might pass: yet the scarfs and the\nbannerets about thee did manifoldly dissuade me from\nbelieving thee a vessel of too great a burthen. I\nhave now found thee; when I lose thee again, I care\nnot: yet art thou good for nothing but taking up; and\nthat thou't scarce worth.","sourceLine":"2.3.208","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.3:87"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hadst thou not the privilege of antiquity upon thee,--","sourceLine":"2.3.216","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.3:88"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Do not plunge thyself too far in anger, lest thou\nhasten thy trial; which if--Lord have mercy on thee\nfor a hen! So, my good window of lattice, fare thee\nwell: thy casement I need not open, for I look\nthrough thee. Give me thy hand.","sourceLine":"2.3.217","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.3:89"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord, you give me most egregious indignity.","sourceLine":"2.3.222","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.3:90"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, with all my heart; and thou art worthy of it.","sourceLine":"2.3.223","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.3:91"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I have not, my lord, deserved it.","sourceLine":"2.3.224","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.3:92"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes, good faith, every dram of it; and I will not\nbate thee a scruple.","sourceLine":"2.3.225","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.3:93"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well, I shall be wiser.","sourceLine":"2.3.227","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.3:94"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Even as soon as thou canst, for thou hast to pull at\na smack o' the contrary. If ever thou be'st bound\nin thy scarf and beaten, thou shalt find what it is\nto be proud of thy bondage. I have a desire to hold\nmy acquaintance with thee, or rather my knowledge,\nthat I may say in the default, he is a man I know.","sourceLine":"2.3.228","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.3:95"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord, you do me most insupportable vexation.","sourceLine":"2.3.234","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.3:96"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I would it were hell-pains for thy sake, and my poor\ndoing eternal: for doing I am past: as I will by\nthee, in what motion age will give me leave.","sourceLine":"2.3.235","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.3:97"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"allswell:2.3:98"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well, thou hast a son shall take this disgrace off\nme; scurvy, old, filthy, scurvy lord! Well, I must\nbe patient; there is no fettering of authority.\nI'll beat him, by my life, if I can meet him with\nany convenience, an he were double and double a\nlord. I'll have no more pity of his age than I\nwould of--I'll beat him, an if I could but meet him again.","sourceLine":"2.3.238","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.3:99"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Re-enter LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.3:100"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sirrah, your lord and master's married; there's news\nfor you: you have a new mistress.","sourceLine":"2.3.245","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.3:101"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I most unfeignedly beseech your lordship to make\nsome reservation of your wrongs: he is my good\nlord: whom I serve above is my master.","sourceLine":"2.3.247","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.3:102"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Who? God?","sourceLine":"2.3.250","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.3:103"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, sir.","sourceLine":"2.3.251","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.3:104"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The devil it is that's thy master. Why dost thou\ngarter up thy arms o' this fashion? dost make hose of\nsleeves? do other servants so? Thou wert best set\nthy lower part where thy nose stands. By mine\nhonour, if I were but two hours younger, I'ld beat\nthee: methinks, thou art a general offence, and\nevery man should beat thee: I think thou wast\ncreated for men to breathe themselves upon thee.","sourceLine":"2.3.252","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.3:105"},{"kind":"speech","text":"This is hard and undeserved measure, my lord.","sourceLine":"2.3.260","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.3:106"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Go to, sir; you were beaten in Italy for picking a\nkernel out of a pomegranate; you are a vagabond and\nno true traveller: you are more saucy with lords\nand honourable personages than the commission of your\nbirth and virtue gives you heraldry. You are not\nworth another word, else I'ld call you knave. I leave you.","sourceLine":"2.3.261","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.3:107"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"allswell:2.3:108"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Good, very good; it is so then: good, very good;\nlet it be concealed awhile.","sourceLine":"2.3.267","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.3:109"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Re-enter BERTRAM","id":"allswell:2.3:110"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Undone, and forfeited to cares for ever!","sourceLine":"2.3.269","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:2.3:111"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What's the matter, sweet-heart?","sourceLine":"2.3.270","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.3:112"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Although before the solemn priest I have sworn,\nI will not bed her.","sourceLine":"2.3.271","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:2.3:113"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What, what, sweet-heart?","sourceLine":"2.3.273","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.3:114"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O my Parolles, they have married me!\nI'll to the Tuscan wars, and never bed her.","sourceLine":"2.3.274","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:2.3:115"},{"kind":"speech","text":"France is a dog-hole, and it no more merits\nThe tread of a man's foot: to the wars!","sourceLine":"2.3.276","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.3:116"},{"kind":"speech","text":"There's letters from my mother: what the import is,\nI know not yet.","sourceLine":"2.3.278","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:2.3:117"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, that would be known. To the wars, my boy, to the wars!\nHe wears his honour in a box unseen,\nThat hugs his kicky-wicky here at home,\nSpending his manly marrow in her arms,\nWhich should sustain the bound and high curvet\nOf Mars's fiery steed. To other regions\nFrance is a stable; we that dwell in't jades;\nTherefore, to the war!","sourceLine":"2.3.280","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.3:118"},{"kind":"speech","text":"It shall be so: I'll send her to my house,\nAcquaint my mother with my hate to her,\nAnd wherefore I am fled; write to the king\nThat which I durst not speak; his present gift\nShall furnish me to those Italian fields,\nWhere noble fellows strike: war is no strife\nTo the dark house and the detested wife.","sourceLine":"2.3.288","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:2.3:119"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Will this capriccio hold in thee? art sure?","sourceLine":"2.3.295","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.3:120"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Go with me to my chamber, and advise me.\nI'll send her straight away: to-morrow\nI'll to the wars, she to her single sorrow.","sourceLine":"2.3.296","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:2.3:121"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, these balls bound; there's noise in it. 'Tis hard:\nA young man married is a man that's marr'd:\nTherefore away, and leave her bravely; go:\nThe king has done you wrong: but, hush, 'tis so.","sourceLine":"2.3.299","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.3:122"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"allswell:2.3:123"}],"scene":3},{"id":"2.4","label":"Act 2 · SCENE IV. Paris. The KING's palace.","act":2,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/allswell/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter HELENA and Clown","id":"allswell:2.4:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My mother greets me kindly; is she well?","sourceLine":"2.4.1","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:2.4:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"She is not well; but yet she has her health: she's\nvery merry; but yet she is not well: but thanks be\ngiven, she's very well and wants nothing i', the\nworld; but yet she is not well.","sourceLine":"2.4.2","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:2.4:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If she be very well, what does she ail, that she's\nnot very well?","sourceLine":"2.4.6","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:2.4:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Truly, she's very well indeed, but for two things.","sourceLine":"2.4.8","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:2.4:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What two things?","sourceLine":"2.4.9","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:2.4:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"One, that she's not in heaven, whither God send her\nquickly! the other that she's in earth, from whence\nGod send her quickly!","sourceLine":"2.4.10","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:2.4:7"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.4:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Bless you, my fortunate lady!","sourceLine":"2.4.13","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.4:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I hope, sir, I have your good will to have mine own\ngood fortunes.","sourceLine":"2.4.14","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:2.4:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You had my prayers to lead them on; and to keep them\non, have them still. O, my knave, how does my old lady?","sourceLine":"2.4.16","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.4:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So that you had her wrinkles and I her money,\nI would she did as you say.","sourceLine":"2.4.18","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:2.4:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, I say nothing.","sourceLine":"2.4.20","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.4:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Marry, you are the wiser man; for many a man's\ntongue shakes out his master's undoing: to say\nnothing, to do nothing, to know nothing, and to have\nnothing, is to be a great part of your title; which\nis within a very little of nothing.","sourceLine":"2.4.21","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:2.4:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Away! thou'rt a knave.","sourceLine":"2.4.26","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.4:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You should have said, sir, before a knave thou'rt a\nknave; that's, before me thou'rt a knave: this had\nbeen truth, sir.","sourceLine":"2.4.27","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:2.4:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Go to, thou art a witty fool; I have found thee.","sourceLine":"2.4.30","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.4:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Did you find me in yourself, sir? or were you\ntaught to find me? The search, sir, was profitable;\nand much fool may you find in you, even to the\nworld's pleasure and the increase of laughter.","sourceLine":"2.4.31","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:2.4:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A good knave, i' faith, and well fed.\nMadam, my lord will go away to-night;\nA very serious business calls on him.\nThe great prerogative and rite of love,\nWhich, as your due, time claims, he does acknowledge;\nBut puts it off to a compell'd restraint;\nWhose want, and whose delay, is strew'd with sweets,\nWhich they distil now in the curbed time,\nTo make the coming hour o'erflow with joy\nAnd pleasure drown the brim.","sourceLine":"2.4.35","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.4:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What's his will else?","sourceLine":"2.4.45","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:2.4:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That you will take your instant leave o' the king\nAnd make this haste as your own good proceeding,\nStrengthen'd with what apology you think\nMay make it probable need.","sourceLine":"2.4.46","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.4:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What more commands he?","sourceLine":"2.4.50","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:2.4:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That, having this obtain'd, you presently\nAttend his further pleasure.","sourceLine":"2.4.51","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.4:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"In every thing I wait upon his will.","sourceLine":"2.4.53","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:2.4:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I shall report it so.","sourceLine":"2.4.54","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.4:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I pray you.","sourceLine":"2.4.55","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:2.4:26"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.4:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come, sirrah.","sourceLine":"2.4.56","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:2.4:28"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"allswell:2.4:29"}],"scene":4},{"id":"2.5","label":"Act 2 · SCENE V. Paris. The KING's palace.","act":2,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/allswell/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter LAFEU and BERTRAM","id":"allswell:2.5:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But I hope your lordship thinks not him a soldier.","sourceLine":"2.5.1","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.5:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes, my lord, and of very valiant approof.","sourceLine":"2.5.2","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:2.5:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You have it from his own deliverance.","sourceLine":"2.5.3","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.5:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And by other warranted testimony.","sourceLine":"2.5.4","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:2.5:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Then my dial goes not true: I took this lark for a bunting.","sourceLine":"2.5.5","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.5:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I do assure you, my lord, he is very great in\nknowledge and accordingly valiant.","sourceLine":"2.5.6","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:2.5:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I have then sinned against his experience and\ntransgressed against his valour; and my state that\nway is dangerous, since I cannot yet find in my\nheart to repent. Here he comes: I pray you, make\nus friends; I will pursue the amity.","sourceLine":"2.5.8","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.5:8"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.5:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[To BERTRAM] These things shall be done, sir.","sourceLine":"2.5.13","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.5:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Pray you, sir, who's his tailor?","sourceLine":"2.5.14","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.5:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sir?","sourceLine":"2.5.15","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.5:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, I know him well, I, sir; he, sir, 's a good\nworkman, a very good tailor.","sourceLine":"2.5.16","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.5:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside to PAROLLES] Is she gone to the king?","sourceLine":"2.5.18","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:2.5:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"She is.","sourceLine":"2.5.19","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.5:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Will she away to-night?","sourceLine":"2.5.20","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:2.5:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"As you'll have her.","sourceLine":"2.5.21","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.5:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I have writ my letters, casketed my treasure,\nGiven order for our horses; and to-night,\nWhen I should take possession of the bride,\nEnd ere I do begin.","sourceLine":"2.5.22","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:2.5:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A good traveller is something at the latter end of a\ndinner; but one that lies three thirds and uses a\nknown truth to pass a thousand nothings with, should\nbe once heard and thrice beaten. God save you, captain.","sourceLine":"2.5.26","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.5:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Is there any unkindness between my lord and you, monsieur?","sourceLine":"2.5.30","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:2.5:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I know not how I have deserved to run into my lord's\ndispleasure.","sourceLine":"2.5.31","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.5:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You have made shift to run into 't, boots and spurs\nand all, like him that leaped into the custard; and\nout of it you'll run again, rather than suffer\nquestion for your residence.","sourceLine":"2.5.33","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.5:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"It may be you have mistaken him, my lord.","sourceLine":"2.5.37","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:2.5:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And shall do so ever, though I took him at 's\nprayers. Fare you well, my lord; and believe this\nof me, there can be no kernel in this light nut; the\nsoul of this man is his clothes. Trust him not in\nmatter of heavy consequence; I have kept of them\ntame, and know their natures. Farewell, monsieur:\nI have spoken better of you than you have or will to\ndeserve at my hand; but we must do good against evil.","sourceLine":"2.5.38","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:2.5:24"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"allswell:2.5:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"An idle lord. I swear.","sourceLine":"2.5.46","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.5:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I think so.","sourceLine":"2.5.47","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:2.5:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, do you not know him?","sourceLine":"2.5.48","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.5:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes, I do know him well, and common speech\nGives him a worthy pass. Here comes my clog.","sourceLine":"2.5.49","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:2.5:29"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter HELENA","id":"allswell:2.5:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I have, sir, as I was commanded from you,\nSpoke with the king and have procured his leave\nFor present parting; only he desires\nSome private speech with you.","sourceLine":"2.5.51","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:2.5:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I shall obey his will.\nYou must not marvel, Helen, at my course,\nWhich holds not colour with the time, nor does\nThe ministration and required office\nOn my particular. Prepared I was not\nFor such a business; therefore am I found\nSo much unsettled: this drives me to entreat you\nThat presently you take our way for home;\nAnd rather muse than ask why I entreat you,\nFor my respects are better than they seem\nAnd my appointments have in them a need\nGreater than shows itself at the first view\nTo you that know them not. This to my mother:","sourceLine":"2.5.55","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:2.5:32"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Giving a letter","id":"allswell:2.5:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'Twill be two days ere I shall see you, so\nI leave you to your wisdom.","sourceLine":"2.5.68","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:2.5:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sir, I can nothing say,\nBut that I am your most obedient servant.","sourceLine":"2.5.70","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:2.5:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come, come, no more of that.","sourceLine":"2.5.72","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:2.5:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And ever shall\nWith true observance seek to eke out that\nWherein toward me my homely stars have fail'd\nTo equal my great fortune.","sourceLine":"2.5.73","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:2.5:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let that go:\nMy haste is very great: farewell; hie home.","sourceLine":"2.5.77","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:2.5:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Pray, sir, your pardon.","sourceLine":"2.5.79","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:2.5:39"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well, what would you say?","sourceLine":"2.5.80","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:2.5:40"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I am not worthy of the wealth I owe,\nNor dare I say 'tis mine, and yet it is;\nBut, like a timorous thief, most fain would steal\nWhat law does vouch mine own.","sourceLine":"2.5.81","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:2.5:41"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What would you have?","sourceLine":"2.5.85","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:2.5:42"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Something; and scarce so much: nothing, indeed.\nI would not tell you what I would, my lord:\nFaith yes;\nStrangers and foes do sunder, and not kiss.","sourceLine":"2.5.86","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:2.5:43"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I pray you, stay not, but in haste to horse.","sourceLine":"2.5.90","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:2.5:44"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I shall not break your bidding, good my lord.","sourceLine":"2.5.91","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:2.5:45"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Where are my other men, monsieur? Farewell.","sourceLine":"2.5.92","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:2.5:46"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit HELENA","id":"allswell:2.5:47"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Go thou toward home; where I will never come\nWhilst I can shake my sword or hear the drum.\nAway, and for our flight.","sourceLine":"2.5.93","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:2.5:48"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Bravely, coragio!","sourceLine":"2.5.96","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:2.5:49"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"allswell:2.5:50"}],"scene":5},{"id":"3.1","label":"Act 3 · SCENE I. Florence. The DUKE's palace.","act":3,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/allswell/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Flourish. Enter the DUKE of Florence attended; the two Frenchmen, with a troop of soldiers.","id":"allswell:3.1:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So that from point to point now have you heard\nThe fundamental reasons of this war,\nWhose great decision hath much blood let forth\nAnd more thirsts after.","sourceLine":"3.1.1","speaker":"DUKE","id":"allswell:3.1:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Holy seems the quarrel\nUpon your grace's part; black and fearful\nOn the opposer.","sourceLine":"3.1.5","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:3.1:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Therefore we marvel much our cousin France\nWould in so just a business shut his bosom\nAgainst our borrowing prayers.","sourceLine":"3.1.8","speaker":"DUKE","id":"allswell:3.1:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Good my lord,\nThe reasons of our state I cannot yield,\nBut like a common and an outward man,\nThat the great figure of a council frames\nBy self-unable motion: therefore dare not\nSay what I think of it, since I have found\nMyself in my incertain grounds to fail\nAs often as I guess'd.","sourceLine":"3.1.11","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:3.1:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Be it his pleasure.","sourceLine":"3.1.19","speaker":"DUKE","id":"allswell:3.1:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But I am sure the younger of our nature,\nThat surfeit on their ease, will day by day\nCome here for physic.","sourceLine":"3.1.20","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:3.1:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Welcome shall they be;\nAnd all the honours that can fly from us\nShall on them settle. You know your places well;\nWhen better fall, for your avails they fell:\nTo-morrow to the field.","sourceLine":"3.1.23","speaker":"DUKE","id":"allswell:3.1:8"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Flourish. Exeunt","id":"allswell:3.1:9"}],"scene":1},{"id":"3.2","label":"Act 3 · SCENE II. Rousillon. The COUNT's palace.","act":3,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/allswell/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter COUNTESS and Clown","id":"allswell:3.2:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"It hath happened all as I would have had it, save\nthat he comes not along with her.","sourceLine":"3.2.1","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:3.2:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"By my troth, I take my young lord to be a very\nmelancholy man.","sourceLine":"3.2.3","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:3.2:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"By what observance, I pray you?","sourceLine":"3.2.5","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:3.2:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, he will look upon his boot and sing; mend the\nruff and sing; ask questions and sing; pick his\nteeth and sing. I know a man that had this trick of\nmelancholy sold a goodly manor for a song.","sourceLine":"3.2.6","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:3.2:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let me see what he writes, and when he means to come.","sourceLine":"3.2.10","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:3.2:6"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Opening a letter","id":"allswell:3.2:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I have no mind to Isbel since I was at court: our\nold ling and our Isbels o' the country are nothing\nlike your old ling and your Isbels o' the court:\nthe brains of my Cupid's knocked out, and I begin to\nlove, as an old man loves money, with no stomach.","sourceLine":"3.2.11","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:3.2:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What have we here?","sourceLine":"3.2.16","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:3.2:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"E'en that you have there.","sourceLine":"3.2.17","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:3.2:10"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"allswell:3.2:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Reads] I have sent you a daughter-in-law: she hath\nrecovered the king, and undone me. I have wedded\nher, not bedded her; and sworn to make the 'not'\neternal. You shall hear I am run away: know it\nbefore the report come. If there be breadth enough\nin the world, I will hold a long distance. My duty\nto you. Your unfortunate son,\nBERTRAM.\nThis is not well, rash and unbridled boy.\nTo fly the favours of so good a king;\nTo pluck his indignation on thy head\nBy the misprising of a maid too virtuous\nFor the contempt of empire.","sourceLine":"3.2.18","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:3.2:12"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Re-enter Clown","id":"allswell:3.2:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O madam, yonder is heavy news within between two\nsoldiers and my young lady!","sourceLine":"3.2.31","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:3.2:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What is the matter?","sourceLine":"3.2.33","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:3.2:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, there is some comfort in the news, some\ncomfort; your son will not be killed so soon as I\nthought he would.","sourceLine":"3.2.34","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:3.2:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why should he be killed?","sourceLine":"3.2.37","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:3.2:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So say I, madam, if he run away, as I hear he does:\nthe danger is in standing to't; that's the loss of\nmen, though it be the getting of children. Here\nthey come will tell you more: for my part, I only\nhear your son was run away.","sourceLine":"3.2.38","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:3.2:18"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"allswell:3.2:19"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter HELENA, and two Gentlemen","id":"allswell:3.2:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Save you, good madam.","sourceLine":"3.2.43","speaker":"First Gentleman","id":"allswell:3.2:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Madam, my lord is gone, for ever gone.","sourceLine":"3.2.44","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:3.2:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Do not say so.","sourceLine":"3.2.45","speaker":"Second Gentleman","id":"allswell:3.2:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Think upon patience. Pray you, gentlemen,\nI have felt so many quirks of joy and grief,\nThat the first face of neither, on the start,\nCan woman me unto't: where is my son, I pray you?","sourceLine":"3.2.46","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:3.2:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Madam, he's gone to serve the duke of Florence:\nWe met him thitherward; for thence we came,\nAnd, after some dispatch in hand at court,\nThither we bend again.","sourceLine":"3.2.50","speaker":"Second Gentleman","id":"allswell:3.2:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Look on his letter, madam; here's my passport.","sourceLine":"3.2.54","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:3.2:26"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Reads","id":"allswell:3.2:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"When thou canst get the ring upon my finger which\nnever shall come off, and show me a child begotten\nof thy body that I am father to, then call me\nhusband: but in such a 'then' I write a 'never.'\nThis is a dreadful sentence.","sourceLine":"3.2.55","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:3.2:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Brought you this letter, gentlemen?","sourceLine":"3.2.60","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:3.2:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, madam;\nAnd for the contents' sake are sorry for our pain.","sourceLine":"3.2.61","speaker":"First Gentleman","id":"allswell:3.2:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I prithee, lady, have a better cheer;\nIf thou engrossest all the griefs are thine,\nThou robb'st me of a moiety: he was my son;\nBut I do wash his name out of my blood,\nAnd thou art all my child. Towards Florence is he?","sourceLine":"3.2.63","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:3.2:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, madam.","sourceLine":"3.2.68","speaker":"Second Gentleman","id":"allswell:3.2:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And to be a soldier?","sourceLine":"3.2.69","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:3.2:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Such is his noble purpose; and believe 't,\nThe duke will lay upon him all the honour\nThat good convenience claims.","sourceLine":"3.2.70","speaker":"Second Gentleman","id":"allswell:3.2:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Return you thither?","sourceLine":"3.2.73","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:3.2:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, madam, with the swiftest wing of speed.","sourceLine":"3.2.74","speaker":"First Gentleman","id":"allswell:3.2:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Reads] Till I have no wife I have nothing in France.\n'Tis bitter.","sourceLine":"3.2.75","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:3.2:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Find you that there?","sourceLine":"3.2.77","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:3.2:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, madam.","sourceLine":"3.2.78","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:3.2:39"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'Tis but the boldness of his hand, haply, which his\nheart was not consenting to.","sourceLine":"3.2.79","speaker":"First Gentleman","id":"allswell:3.2:40"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nothing in France, until he have no wife!\nThere's nothing here that is too good for him\nBut only she; and she deserves a lord\nThat twenty such rude boys might tend upon\nAnd call her hourly mistress. Who was with him?","sourceLine":"3.2.81","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:3.2:41"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A servant only, and a gentleman\nWhich I have sometime known.","sourceLine":"3.2.86","speaker":"First Gentleman","id":"allswell:3.2:42"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Parolles, was it not?","sourceLine":"3.2.88","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:3.2:43"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, my good lady, he.","sourceLine":"3.2.89","speaker":"First Gentleman","id":"allswell:3.2:44"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A very tainted fellow, and full of wickedness.\nMy son corrupts a well-derived nature\nWith his inducement.","sourceLine":"3.2.90","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:3.2:45"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Indeed, good lady,\nThe fellow has a deal of that too much,\nWhich holds him much to have.","sourceLine":"3.2.93","speaker":"First Gentleman","id":"allswell:3.2:46"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You're welcome, gentlemen.\nI will entreat you, when you see my son,\nTo tell him that his sword can never win\nThe honour that he loses: more I'll entreat you\nWritten to bear along.","sourceLine":"3.2.96","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:3.2:47"},{"kind":"speech","text":"We serve you, madam,\nIn that and all your worthiest affairs.","sourceLine":"3.2.101","speaker":"Second Gentleman","id":"allswell:3.2:48"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Not so, but as we change our courtesies.\nWill you draw near!","sourceLine":"3.2.103","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:3.2:49"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt COUNTESS and Gentlemen","id":"allswell:3.2:50"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'Till I have no wife, I have nothing in France.'\nNothing in France, until he has no wife!\nThou shalt have none, Rousillon, none in France;\nThen hast thou all again. Poor lord! is't I\nThat chase thee from thy country and expose\nThose tender limbs of thine to the event\nOf the none-sparing war? and is it I\nThat drive thee from the sportive court, where thou\nWast shot at with fair eyes, to be the mark\nOf smoky muskets? O you leaden messengers,\nThat ride upon the violent speed of fire,\nFly with false aim; move the still-peering air,\nThat sings with piercing; do not touch my lord.\nWhoever shoots at him, I set him there;\nWhoever charges on his forward breast,\nI am the caitiff that do hold him to't;\nAnd, though I kill him not, I am the cause\nHis death was so effected: better 'twere\nI met the ravin lion when he roar'd\nWith sharp constraint of hunger; better 'twere\nThat all the miseries which nature owes\nWere mine at once. No, come thou home, Rousillon,\nWhence honour but of danger wins a scar,\nAs oft it loses all: I will be gone;\nMy being here it is that holds thee hence:\nShall I stay here to do't? no, no, although\nThe air of paradise did fan the house\nAnd angels officed all: I will be gone,\nThat pitiful rumour may report my flight,\nTo consolate thine ear. Come, night; end, day!\nFor with the dark, poor thief, I'll steal away.","sourceLine":"3.2.105","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:3.2:51"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"allswell:3.2:52"}],"scene":2},{"id":"3.3","label":"Act 3 · SCENE III. Florence. Before the DUKE's palace.","act":3,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/allswell/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Flourish. Enter the DUKE of Florence, BERTRAM, PAROLLES, Soldiers, Drum, and Trumpets","id":"allswell:3.3:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The general of our horse thou art; and we,\nGreat in our hope, lay our best love and credence\nUpon thy promising fortune.","sourceLine":"3.3.1","speaker":"DUKE","id":"allswell:3.3:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sir, it is\nA charge too heavy for my strength, but yet\nWe'll strive to bear it for your worthy sake\nTo the extreme edge of hazard.","sourceLine":"3.3.4","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:3.3:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Then go thou forth;\nAnd fortune play upon thy prosperous helm,\nAs thy auspicious mistress!","sourceLine":"3.3.8","speaker":"DUKE","id":"allswell:3.3:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"This very day,\nGreat Mars, I put myself into thy file:\nMake me but like my thoughts, and I shall prove\nA lover of thy drum, hater of love.","sourceLine":"3.3.11","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:3.3:5"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"allswell:3.3:6"}],"scene":3},{"id":"3.4","label":"Act 3 · SCENE IV. Rousillon. The COUNT's palace.","act":3,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/allswell/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter COUNTESS and Steward","id":"allswell:3.4:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Alas! and would you take the letter of her?\nMight you not know she would do as she has done,\nBy sending me a letter? Read it again.","sourceLine":"3.4.1","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:3.4:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Reads]\nI am Saint Jaques' pilgrim, thither gone:\nAmbitious love hath so in me offended,\nThat barefoot plod I the cold ground upon,\nWith sainted vow my faults to have amended.\nWrite, write, that from the bloody course of war\nMy dearest master, your dear son, may hie:\nBless him at home in peace, whilst I from far\nHis name with zealous fervor sanctify:\nHis taken labours bid him me forgive;\nI, his despiteful Juno, sent him forth\nFrom courtly friends, with camping foes to live,\nWhere death and danger dogs the heels of worth:\nHe is too good and fair for death and me:\nWhom I myself embrace, to set him free.","sourceLine":"3.4.4","speaker":"Steward","id":"allswell:3.4:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ah, what sharp stings are in her mildest words!\nRinaldo, you did never lack advice so much,\nAs letting her pass so: had I spoke with her,\nI could have well diverted her intents,\nWhich thus she hath prevented.","sourceLine":"3.4.19","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:3.4:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Pardon me, madam:\nIf I had given you this at over-night,\nShe might have been o'erta'en; and yet she writes,\nPursuit would be but vain.","sourceLine":"3.4.24","speaker":"Steward","id":"allswell:3.4:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What angel shall\nBless this unworthy husband? he cannot thrive,\nUnless her prayers, whom heaven delights to hear\nAnd loves to grant, reprieve him from the wrath\nOf greatest justice. Write, write, Rinaldo,\nTo this unworthy husband of his wife;\nLet every word weigh heavy of her worth\nThat he does weigh too light: my greatest grief.\nThough little he do feel it, set down sharply.\nDispatch the most convenient messenger:\nWhen haply he shall hear that she is gone,\nHe will return; and hope I may that she,\nHearing so much, will speed her foot again,\nLed hither by pure love: which of them both\nIs dearest to me. I have no skill in sense\nTo make distinction: provide this messenger:\nMy heart is heavy and mine age is weak;\nGrief would have tears, and sorrow bids me speak.","sourceLine":"3.4.28","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:3.4:6"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"allswell:3.4:7"}],"scene":4},{"id":"3.5","label":"Act 3 · SCENE V. Florence. Without the walls. A tucket afar off.","act":3,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/allswell/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter an old Widow of Florence, DIANA, VIOLENTA, and MARIANA, with other Citizens","id":"allswell:3.5:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, come; for if they do approach the city, we\nshall lose all the sight.","sourceLine":"3.5.1","speaker":"Widow","id":"allswell:3.5:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"They say the French count has done most honourable service.","sourceLine":"3.5.3","speaker":"DIANA","id":"allswell:3.5:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"It is reported that he has taken their greatest\ncommander; and that with his own hand he slew the\nduke's brother.","sourceLine":"3.5.4","speaker":"Widow","id":"allswell:3.5:4"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Tucket","id":"allswell:3.5:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"We have lost our labour; they are gone a contrary\nway: hark! you may know by their trumpets.","sourceLine":"3.5.7","speaker":"Widow","id":"allswell:3.5:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come, let's return again, and suffice ourselves with\nthe report of it. Well, Diana, take heed of this\nFrench earl: the honour of a maid is her name; and\nno legacy is so rich as honesty.","sourceLine":"3.5.9","speaker":"MARIANA","id":"allswell:3.5:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I have told my neighbour how you have been solicited\nby a gentleman his companion.","sourceLine":"3.5.13","speaker":"Widow","id":"allswell:3.5:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I know that knave; hang him! one Parolles: a\nfilthy officer he is in those suggestions for the\nyoung earl. Beware of them, Diana; their promises,\nenticements, oaths, tokens, and all these engines of\nlust, are not the things they go under: many a maid\nhath been seduced by them; and the misery is,\nexample, that so terrible shows in the wreck of\nmaidenhood, cannot for all that dissuade succession,\nbut that they are limed with the twigs that threaten\nthem. I hope I need not to advise you further; but\nI hope your own grace will keep you where you are,\nthough there were no further danger known but the\nmodesty which is so lost.","sourceLine":"3.5.15","speaker":"MARIANA","id":"allswell:3.5:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You shall not need to fear me.","sourceLine":"3.5.28","speaker":"DIANA","id":"allswell:3.5:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I hope so.","sourceLine":"3.5.29","speaker":"Widow","id":"allswell:3.5:11"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter HELENA, disguised like a Pilgrim","id":"allswell:3.5:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Look, here comes a pilgrim: I know she will lie at\nmy house; thither they send one another: I'll\nquestion her. God save you, pilgrim! whither are you bound?","sourceLine":"3.5.30","speaker":"Widow","id":"allswell:3.5:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"To Saint Jaques le Grand.\nWhere do the palmers lodge, I do beseech you?","sourceLine":"3.5.33","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:3.5:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"At the Saint Francis here beside the port.","sourceLine":"3.5.35","speaker":"Widow","id":"allswell:3.5:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Is this the way?","sourceLine":"3.5.36","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:3.5:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, marry, is't.","sourceLine":"3.5.37","speaker":"Widow","id":"allswell:3.5:17"},{"kind":"stage","text":"A march afar","id":"allswell:3.5:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hark you! they come this way.\nIf you will tarry, holy pilgrim,\nBut till the troops come by,\nI will conduct you where you shall be lodged;\nThe rather, for I think I know your hostess\nAs ample as myself.","sourceLine":"3.5.38","speaker":"Widow","id":"allswell:3.5:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Is it yourself?","sourceLine":"3.5.44","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:3.5:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If you shall please so, pilgrim.","sourceLine":"3.5.45","speaker":"Widow","id":"allswell:3.5:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I thank you, and will stay upon your leisure.","sourceLine":"3.5.46","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:3.5:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You came, I think, from France?","sourceLine":"3.5.47","speaker":"Widow","id":"allswell:3.5:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I did so.","sourceLine":"3.5.48","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:3.5:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Here you shall see a countryman of yours\nThat has done worthy service.","sourceLine":"3.5.49","speaker":"Widow","id":"allswell:3.5:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"His name, I pray you.","sourceLine":"3.5.51","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:3.5:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The Count Rousillon: know you such a one?","sourceLine":"3.5.52","speaker":"DIANA","id":"allswell:3.5:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But by the ear, that hears most nobly of him:\nHis face I know not.","sourceLine":"3.5.53","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:3.5:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Whatsome'er he is,\nHe's bravely taken here. He stole from France,\nAs 'tis reported, for the king had married him\nAgainst his liking: think you it is so?","sourceLine":"3.5.55","speaker":"DIANA","id":"allswell:3.5:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, surely, mere the truth: I know his lady.","sourceLine":"3.5.59","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:3.5:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"There is a gentleman that serves the count\nReports but coarsely of her.","sourceLine":"3.5.60","speaker":"DIANA","id":"allswell:3.5:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What's his name?","sourceLine":"3.5.62","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:3.5:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Monsieur Parolles.","sourceLine":"3.5.63","speaker":"DIANA","id":"allswell:3.5:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, I believe with him,\nIn argument of praise, or to the worth\nOf the great count himself, she is too mean\nTo have her name repeated: all her deserving\nIs a reserved honesty, and that\nI have not heard examined.","sourceLine":"3.5.64","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:3.5:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Alas, poor lady!\n'Tis a hard bondage to become the wife\nOf a detesting lord.","sourceLine":"3.5.70","speaker":"DIANA","id":"allswell:3.5:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I warrant, good creature, wheresoe'er she is,\nHer heart weighs sadly: this young maid might do her\nA shrewd turn, if she pleased.","sourceLine":"3.5.73","speaker":"Widow","id":"allswell:3.5:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How do you mean?\nMay be the amorous count solicits her\nIn the unlawful purpose.","sourceLine":"3.5.76","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:3.5:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He does indeed;\nAnd brokes with all that can in such a suit\nCorrupt the tender honour of a maid:\nBut she is arm'd for him and keeps her guard\nIn honestest defence.","sourceLine":"3.5.79","speaker":"Widow","id":"allswell:3.5:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The gods forbid else!","sourceLine":"3.5.84","speaker":"MARIANA","id":"allswell:3.5:39"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So, now they come:","sourceLine":"3.5.85","speaker":"Widow","id":"allswell:3.5:40"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Drum and Colours","id":"allswell:3.5:41"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter BERTRAM, PAROLLES, and the whole army","id":"allswell:3.5:42"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That is Antonio, the duke's eldest son;\nThat, Escalus.","sourceLine":"3.5.86","speaker":"Widow","id":"allswell:3.5:43"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Which is the Frenchman?","sourceLine":"3.5.88","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:3.5:44"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He;\nThat with the plume: 'tis a most gallant fellow.\nI would he loved his wife: if he were honester\nHe were much goodlier: is't not a handsome gentleman?","sourceLine":"3.5.89","speaker":"DIANA","id":"allswell:3.5:45"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I like him well.","sourceLine":"3.5.93","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:3.5:46"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'Tis pity he is not honest: yond's that same knave\nThat leads him to these places: were I his lady,\nI would Poison that vile rascal.","sourceLine":"3.5.94","speaker":"DIANA","id":"allswell:3.5:47"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Which is he?","sourceLine":"3.5.97","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:3.5:48"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That jack-an-apes with scarfs: why is he melancholy?","sourceLine":"3.5.98","speaker":"DIANA","id":"allswell:3.5:49"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Perchance he's hurt i' the battle.","sourceLine":"3.5.99","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:3.5:50"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Lose our drum! well.","sourceLine":"3.5.100","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:3.5:51"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He's shrewdly vexed at something: look, he has spied us.","sourceLine":"3.5.101","speaker":"MARIANA","id":"allswell:3.5:52"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Marry, hang you!","sourceLine":"3.5.102","speaker":"Widow","id":"allswell:3.5:53"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And your courtesy, for a ring-carrier!","sourceLine":"3.5.103","speaker":"MARIANA","id":"allswell:3.5:54"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt BERTRAM, PAROLLES, and army","id":"allswell:3.5:55"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The troop is past. Come, pilgrim, I will bring you\nWhere you shall host: of enjoin'd penitents\nThere's four or five, to great Saint Jaques bound,\nAlready at my house.","sourceLine":"3.5.104","speaker":"Widow","id":"allswell:3.5:56"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I humbly thank you:\nPlease it this matron and this gentle maid\nTo eat with us to-night, the charge and thanking\nShall be for me; and, to requite you further,\nI will bestow some precepts of this virgin\nWorthy the note.","sourceLine":"3.5.108","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:3.5:57"},{"kind":"speech","text":"We'll take your offer kindly.","sourceLine":"3.5.114","speaker":"BOTH","id":"allswell:3.5:58"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"allswell:3.5:59"}],"scene":5},{"id":"3.6","label":"Act 3 · SCENE VI. Camp before Florence.","act":3,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/allswell/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter BERTRAM and the two French Lords","id":"allswell:3.6:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, good my lord, put him to't; let him have his\nway.","sourceLine":"3.6.1","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:3.6:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If your lordship find him not a hilding, hold me no\nmore in your respect.","sourceLine":"3.6.3","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:3.6:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"On my life, my lord, a bubble.","sourceLine":"3.6.5","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:3.6:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Do you think I am so far deceived in him?","sourceLine":"3.6.6","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:3.6:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Believe it, my lord, in mine own direct knowledge,\nwithout any malice, but to speak of him as my\nkinsman, he's a most notable coward, an infinite and\nendless liar, an hourly promise-breaker, the owner\nof no one good quality worthy your lordship's\nentertainment.","sourceLine":"3.6.7","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:3.6:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"It were fit you knew him; lest, reposing too far in\nhis virtue, which he hath not, he might at some\ngreat and trusty business in a main danger fail you.","sourceLine":"3.6.13","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:3.6:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I would I knew in what particular action to try him.","sourceLine":"3.6.16","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:3.6:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"None better than to let him fetch off his drum,\nwhich you hear him so confidently undertake to do.","sourceLine":"3.6.17","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:3.6:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I, with a troop of Florentines, will suddenly\nsurprise him; such I will have, whom I am sure he\nknows not from the enemy: we will bind and hoodwink\nhim so, that he shall suppose no other but that he\nis carried into the leaguer of the adversaries, when\nwe bring him to our own tents. Be but your lordship\npresent at his examination: if he do not, for the\npromise of his life and in the highest compulsion of\nbase fear, offer to betray you and deliver all the\nintelligence in his power against you, and that with\nthe divine forfeit of his soul upon oath, never\ntrust my judgment in any thing.","sourceLine":"3.6.19","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:3.6:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, for the love of laughter, let him fetch his drum;\nhe says he has a stratagem for't: when your\nlordship sees the bottom of his success in't, and to\nwhat metal this counterfeit lump of ore will be\nmelted, if you give him not John Drum's\nentertainment, your inclining cannot be removed.\nHere he comes.","sourceLine":"3.6.31","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:3.6:11"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter PAROLLES","id":"allswell:3.6:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside to BERTRAM] O, for the love of laughter,\nhinder not the honour of his design: let him fetch\noff his drum in any hand.","sourceLine":"3.6.38","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:3.6:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How now, monsieur! this drum sticks sorely in your\ndisposition.","sourceLine":"3.6.41","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:3.6:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A pox on't, let it go; 'tis but a drum.","sourceLine":"3.6.43","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:3.6:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'But a drum'! is't 'but a drum'? A drum so lost!\nThere was excellent command,--to charge in with our\nhorse upon our own wings, and to rend our own soldiers!","sourceLine":"3.6.44","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:3.6:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That was not to be blamed in the command of the\nservice: it was a disaster of war that Caesar\nhimself could not have prevented, if he had been\nthere to command.","sourceLine":"3.6.47","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:3.6:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well, we cannot greatly condemn our success: some\ndishonour we had in the loss of that drum; but it is\nnot to be recovered.","sourceLine":"3.6.51","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:3.6:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"It might have been recovered.","sourceLine":"3.6.54","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:3.6:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"It might; but it is not now.","sourceLine":"3.6.55","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:3.6:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"It is to be recovered: but that the merit of\nservice is seldom attributed to the true and exact\nperformer, I would have that drum or another, or\n'hic jacet.'","sourceLine":"3.6.56","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:3.6:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, if you have a stomach, to't, monsieur: if you\nthink your mystery in stratagem can bring this\ninstrument of honour again into his native quarter,\nbe magnanimous in the enterprise and go on; I will\ngrace the attempt for a worthy exploit: if you\nspeed well in it, the duke shall both speak of it.\nand extend to you what further becomes his\ngreatness, even to the utmost syllable of your\nworthiness.","sourceLine":"3.6.60","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:3.6:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"By the hand of a soldier, I will undertake it.","sourceLine":"3.6.69","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:3.6:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But you must not now slumber in it.","sourceLine":"3.6.70","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:3.6:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I'll about it this evening: and I will presently\npen down my dilemmas, encourage myself in my\ncertainty, put myself into my mortal preparation;\nand by midnight look to hear further from me.","sourceLine":"3.6.71","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:3.6:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"May I be bold to acquaint his grace you are gone about it?","sourceLine":"3.6.75","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:3.6:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I know not what the success will be, my lord; but\nthe attempt I vow.","sourceLine":"3.6.76","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:3.6:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I know thou'rt valiant; and, to the possibility of\nthy soldiership, will subscribe for thee. Farewell.","sourceLine":"3.6.78","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:3.6:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I love not many words.","sourceLine":"3.6.80","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:3.6:29"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"allswell:3.6:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No more than a fish loves water. Is not this a\nstrange fellow, my lord, that so confidently seems\nto undertake this business, which he knows is not to\nbe done; damns himself to do and dares better be\ndamned than to do't?","sourceLine":"3.6.81","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:3.6:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You do not know him, my lord, as we do: certain it\nis that he will steal himself into a man's favour and\nfor a week escape a great deal of discoveries; but\nwhen you find him out, you have him ever after.","sourceLine":"3.6.86","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:3.6:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, do you think he will make no deed at all of\nthis that so seriously he does address himself unto?","sourceLine":"3.6.90","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:3.6:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"None in the world; but return with an invention and\nclap upon you two or three probable lies: but we\nhave almost embossed him; you shall see his fall\nto-night; for indeed he is not for your lordship's respect.","sourceLine":"3.6.92","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:3.6:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"We'll make you some sport with the fox ere we case\nhim. He was first smoked by the old lord Lafeu:\nwhen his disguise and he is parted, tell me what a\nsprat you shall find him; which you shall see this\nvery night.","sourceLine":"3.6.96","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:3.6:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I must go look my twigs: he shall be caught.","sourceLine":"3.6.101","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:3.6:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Your brother he shall go along with me.","sourceLine":"3.6.102","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:3.6:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"As't please your lordship: I'll leave you.","sourceLine":"3.6.103","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:3.6:38"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"allswell:3.6:39"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now will I lead you to the house, and show you\nThe lass I spoke of.","sourceLine":"3.6.104","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:3.6:40"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But you say she's honest.","sourceLine":"3.6.106","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:3.6:41"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That's all the fault: I spoke with her but once\nAnd found her wondrous cold; but I sent to her,\nBy this same coxcomb that we have i' the wind,\nTokens and letters which she did re-send;\nAnd this is all I have done. She's a fair creature:\nWill you go see her?","sourceLine":"3.6.107","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:3.6:42"},{"kind":"speech","text":"With all my heart, my lord.","sourceLine":"3.6.113","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:3.6:43"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"allswell:3.6:44"}],"scene":6},{"id":"3.7","label":"Act 3 · SCENE VII. Florence. The Widow's house.","act":3,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/allswell/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter HELENA and Widow","id":"allswell:3.7:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If you misdoubt me that I am not she,\nI know not how I shall assure you further,\nBut I shall lose the grounds I work upon.","sourceLine":"3.7.1","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:3.7:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Though my estate be fallen, I was well born,\nNothing acquainted with these businesses;\nAnd would not put my reputation now\nIn any staining act.","sourceLine":"3.7.4","speaker":"Widow","id":"allswell:3.7:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nor would I wish you.\nFirst, give me trust, the count he is my husband,\nAnd what to your sworn counsel I have spoken\nIs so from word to word; and then you cannot,\nBy the good aid that I of you shall borrow,\nErr in bestowing it.","sourceLine":"3.7.8","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:3.7:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I should believe you:\nFor you have show'd me that which well approves\nYou're great in fortune.","sourceLine":"3.7.14","speaker":"Widow","id":"allswell:3.7:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Take this purse of gold,\nAnd let me buy your friendly help thus far,\nWhich I will over-pay and pay again\nWhen I have found it. The count he wooes your daughter,\nLays down his wanton siege before her beauty,\nResolved to carry her: let her in fine consent,\nAs we'll direct her how 'tis best to bear it.\nNow his important blood will nought deny\nThat she'll demand: a ring the county wears,\nThat downward hath succeeded in his house\nFrom son to son, some four or five descents\nSince the first father wore it: this ring he holds\nIn most rich choice; yet in his idle fire,\nTo buy his will, it would not seem too dear,\nHowe'er repented after.","sourceLine":"3.7.17","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:3.7:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now I see\nThe bottom of your purpose.","sourceLine":"3.7.32","speaker":"Widow","id":"allswell:3.7:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You see it lawful, then: it is no more,\nBut that your daughter, ere she seems as won,\nDesires this ring; appoints him an encounter;\nIn fine, delivers me to fill the time,\nHerself most chastely absent: after this,\nTo marry her, I'll add three thousand crowns\nTo what is passed already.","sourceLine":"3.7.34","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:3.7:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I have yielded:\nInstruct my daughter how she shall persever,\nThat time and place with this deceit so lawful\nMay prove coherent. Every night he comes\nWith musics of all sorts and songs composed\nTo her unworthiness: it nothing steads us\nTo chide him from our eaves; for he persists\nAs if his life lay on't.","sourceLine":"3.7.41","speaker":"Widow","id":"allswell:3.7:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why then to-night\nLet us assay our plot; which, if it speed,\nIs wicked meaning in a lawful deed\nAnd lawful meaning in a lawful act,\nWhere both not sin, and yet a sinful fact:\nBut let's about it.","sourceLine":"3.7.49","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:3.7:10"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"allswell:3.7:11"}],"scene":7},{"id":"4.1","label":"Act 4 · SCENE I. Without the Florentine camp.","act":4,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/allswell/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Second French Lord, with five or six other Soldiers in ambush","id":"allswell:4.1:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He can come no other way but by this hedge-corner.\nWhen you sally upon him, speak what terrible\nlanguage you will: though you understand it not\nyourselves, no matter; for we must not seem to\nunderstand him, unless some one among us whom we\nmust produce for an interpreter.","sourceLine":"4.1.1","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:4.1:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Good captain, let me be the interpreter.","sourceLine":"4.1.7","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"allswell:4.1:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Art not acquainted with him? knows he not thy voice?","sourceLine":"4.1.8","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:4.1:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No, sir, I warrant you.","sourceLine":"4.1.9","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"allswell:4.1:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But what linsey-woolsey hast thou to speak to us again?","sourceLine":"4.1.10","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:4.1:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"E'en such as you speak to me.","sourceLine":"4.1.11","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"allswell:4.1:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He must think us some band of strangers i' the\nadversary's entertainment. Now he hath a smack of\nall neighbouring languages; therefore we must every\none be a man of his own fancy, not to know what we\nspeak one to another; so we seem to know, is to\nknow straight our purpose: choughs' language,\ngabble enough, and good enough. As for you,\ninterpreter, you must seem very politic. But couch,\nho! here he comes, to beguile two hours in a sleep,\nand then to return and swear the lies he forges.","sourceLine":"4.1.12","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:4.1:8"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter PAROLLES","id":"allswell:4.1:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ten o'clock: within these three hours 'twill be\ntime enough to go home. What shall I say I have\ndone? It must be a very plausive invention that\ncarries it: they begin to smoke me; and disgraces\nhave of late knocked too often at my door. I find\nmy tongue is too foolhardy; but my heart hath the\nfear of Mars before it and of his creatures, not\ndaring the reports of my tongue.","sourceLine":"4.1.22","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:4.1:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"This is the first truth that e'er thine own tongue\nwas guilty of.","sourceLine":"4.1.30","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:4.1:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What the devil should move me to undertake the\nrecovery of this drum, being not ignorant of the\nimpossibility, and knowing I had no such purpose? I\nmust give myself some hurts, and say I got them in\nexploit: yet slight ones will not carry it; they\nwill say, 'Came you off with so little?' and great\nones I dare not give. Wherefore, what's the\ninstance? Tongue, I must put you into a\nbutter-woman's mouth and buy myself another of\nBajazet's mule, if you prattle me into these perils.","sourceLine":"4.1.32","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:4.1:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Is it possible he should know what he is, and be\nthat he is?","sourceLine":"4.1.42","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:4.1:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I would the cutting of my garments would serve the\nturn, or the breaking of my Spanish sword.","sourceLine":"4.1.44","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:4.1:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"We cannot afford you so.","sourceLine":"4.1.46","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:4.1:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Or the baring of my beard; and to say it was in\nstratagem.","sourceLine":"4.1.47","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:4.1:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'Twould not do.","sourceLine":"4.1.49","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:4.1:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Or to drown my clothes, and say I was stripped.","sourceLine":"4.1.50","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:4.1:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hardly serve.","sourceLine":"4.1.51","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:4.1:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Though I swore I leaped from the window of the citadel.","sourceLine":"4.1.52","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:4.1:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How deep?","sourceLine":"4.1.53","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:4.1:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thirty fathom.","sourceLine":"4.1.54","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:4.1:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Three great oaths would scarce make that be believed.","sourceLine":"4.1.55","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:4.1:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I would I had any drum of the enemy's: I would swear\nI recovered it.","sourceLine":"4.1.56","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:4.1:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You shall hear one anon.","sourceLine":"4.1.58","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:4.1:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A drum now of the enemy's,--","sourceLine":"4.1.59","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:4.1:26"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Alarum within","id":"allswell:4.1:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Throca movousus, cargo, cargo, cargo.","sourceLine":"4.1.60","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:4.1:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Cargo, cargo, cargo, villiando par corbo, cargo.","sourceLine":"4.1.61","speaker":"All","id":"allswell:4.1:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, ransom, ransom! do not hide mine eyes.","sourceLine":"4.1.62","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:4.1:30"},{"kind":"stage","text":"They seize and blindfold him","id":"allswell:4.1:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Boskos thromuldo boskos.","sourceLine":"4.1.63","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"allswell:4.1:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I know you are the Muskos' regiment:\nAnd I shall lose my life for want of language;\nIf there be here German, or Dane, low Dutch,\nItalian, or French, let him speak to me; I'll\nDiscover that which shall undo the Florentine.","sourceLine":"4.1.64","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:4.1:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Boskos vauvado: I understand thee, and can speak\nthy tongue. Kerely bonto, sir, betake thee to thy\nfaith, for seventeen poniards are at thy bosom.","sourceLine":"4.1.69","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"allswell:4.1:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O!","sourceLine":"4.1.72","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:4.1:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, pray, pray, pray! Manka revania dulche.","sourceLine":"4.1.73","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"allswell:4.1:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Oscorbidulchos volivorco.","sourceLine":"4.1.74","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:4.1:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The general is content to spare thee yet;\nAnd, hoodwink'd as thou art, will lead thee on\nTo gather from thee: haply thou mayst inform\nSomething to save thy life.","sourceLine":"4.1.75","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"allswell:4.1:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O, let me live!\nAnd all the secrets of our camp I'll show,\nTheir force, their purposes; nay, I'll speak that\nWhich you will wonder at.","sourceLine":"4.1.79","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:4.1:39"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But wilt thou faithfully?","sourceLine":"4.1.83","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"allswell:4.1:40"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If I do not, damn me.","sourceLine":"4.1.84","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:4.1:41"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Acordo linta.\nCome on; thou art granted space.","sourceLine":"4.1.85","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"allswell:4.1:42"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit, with PAROLLES guarded. A short alarum within","id":"allswell:4.1:43"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Go, tell the Count Rousillon, and my brother,\nWe have caught the woodcock, and will keep him muffled\nTill we do hear from them.","sourceLine":"4.1.87","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:4.1:44"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Captain, I will.","sourceLine":"4.1.90","speaker":"Second Soldier","id":"allswell:4.1:45"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A' will betray us all unto ourselves:\nInform on that.","sourceLine":"4.1.91","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:4.1:46"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So I will, sir.","sourceLine":"4.1.93","speaker":"Second Soldier","id":"allswell:4.1:47"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Till then I'll keep him dark and safely lock'd.","sourceLine":"4.1.94","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:4.1:48"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"allswell:4.1:49"}],"scene":1},{"id":"4.2","label":"Act 4 · SCENE II. Florence. The Widow's house.","act":4,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/allswell/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter BERTRAM and DIANA","id":"allswell:4.2:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"They told me that your name was Fontibell.","sourceLine":"4.2.1","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:4.2:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No, my good lord, Diana.","sourceLine":"4.2.2","speaker":"DIANA","id":"allswell:4.2:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Titled goddess;\nAnd worth it, with addition! But, fair soul,\nIn your fine frame hath love no quality?\nIf quick fire of youth light not your mind,\nYou are no maiden, but a monument:\nWhen you are dead, you should be such a one\nAs you are now, for you are cold and stem;\nAnd now you should be as your mother was\nWhen your sweet self was got.","sourceLine":"4.2.3","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:4.2:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"She then was honest.","sourceLine":"4.2.12","speaker":"DIANA","id":"allswell:4.2:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So should you be.","sourceLine":"4.2.13","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:4.2:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No:\nMy mother did but duty; such, my lord,\nAs you owe to your wife.","sourceLine":"4.2.14","speaker":"DIANA","id":"allswell:4.2:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No more o' that;\nI prithee, do not strive against my vows:\nI was compell'd to her; but I love thee\nBy love's own sweet constraint, and will for ever\nDo thee all rights of service.","sourceLine":"4.2.17","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:4.2:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, so you serve us\nTill we serve you; but when you have our roses,\nYou barely leave our thorns to prick ourselves\nAnd mock us with our bareness.","sourceLine":"4.2.22","speaker":"DIANA","id":"allswell:4.2:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How have I sworn!","sourceLine":"4.2.26","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:4.2:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'Tis not the many oaths that makes the truth,\nBut the plain single vow that is vow'd true.\nWhat is not holy, that we swear not by,\nBut take the High'st to witness: then, pray you, tell me,\nIf I should swear by God's great attributes,\nI loved you dearly, would you believe my oaths,\nWhen I did love you ill? This has no holding,\nTo swear by him whom I protest to love,\nThat I will work against him: therefore your oaths\nAre words and poor conditions, but unseal'd,\nAt least in my opinion.","sourceLine":"4.2.27","speaker":"DIANA","id":"allswell:4.2:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Change it, change it;\nBe not so holy-cruel: love is holy;\nAnd my integrity ne'er knew the crafts\nThat you do charge men with. Stand no more off,\nBut give thyself unto my sick desires,\nWho then recover: say thou art mine, and ever\nMy love as it begins shall so persever.","sourceLine":"4.2.38","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:4.2:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I see that men make ropes in such a scarre\nThat we'll forsake ourselves. Give me that ring.","sourceLine":"4.2.45","speaker":"DIANA","id":"allswell:4.2:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I'll lend it thee, my dear; but have no power\nTo give it from me.","sourceLine":"4.2.47","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:4.2:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Will you not, my lord?","sourceLine":"4.2.49","speaker":"DIANA","id":"allswell:4.2:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"It is an honour 'longing to our house,\nBequeathed down from many ancestors;\nWhich were the greatest obloquy i' the world\nIn me to lose.","sourceLine":"4.2.50","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:4.2:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Mine honour's such a ring:\nMy chastity's the jewel of our house,\nBequeathed down from many ancestors;\nWhich were the greatest obloquy i' the world\nIn me to lose: thus your own proper wisdom\nBrings in the champion Honour on my part,\nAgainst your vain assault.","sourceLine":"4.2.54","speaker":"DIANA","id":"allswell:4.2:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Here, take my ring:\nMy house, mine honour, yea, my life, be thine,\nAnd I'll be bid by thee.","sourceLine":"4.2.61","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:4.2:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"When midnight comes, knock at my chamber-window:\nI'll order take my mother shall not hear.\nNow will I charge you in the band of truth,\nWhen you have conquer'd my yet maiden bed,\nRemain there but an hour, nor speak to me:\nMy reasons are most strong; and you shall know them\nWhen back again this ring shall be deliver'd:\nAnd on your finger in the night I'll put\nAnother ring, that what in time proceeds\nMay token to the future our past deeds.\nAdieu, till then; then, fail not. You have won\nA wife of me, though there my hope be done.","sourceLine":"4.2.64","speaker":"DIANA","id":"allswell:4.2:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A heaven on earth I have won by wooing thee.","sourceLine":"4.2.76","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:4.2:20"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"allswell:4.2:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"For which live long to thank both heaven and me!\nYou may so in the end.\nMy mother told me just how he would woo,\nAs if she sat in 's heart; she says all men\nHave the like oaths: he had sworn to marry me\nWhen his wife's dead; therefore I'll lie with him\nWhen I am buried. Since Frenchmen are so braid,\nMarry that will, I live and die a maid:\nOnly in this disguise I think't no sin\nTo cozen him that would unjustly win.","sourceLine":"4.2.77","speaker":"DIANA","id":"allswell:4.2:22"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"allswell:4.2:23"}],"scene":2},{"id":"4.3","label":"Act 4 · SCENE III. The Florentine camp.","act":4,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/allswell/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter the two French Lords and some two or three Soldiers","id":"allswell:4.3:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You have not given him his mother's letter?","sourceLine":"4.3.1","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I have delivered it an hour since: there is\nsomething in't that stings his nature; for on the\nreading it he changed almost into another man.","sourceLine":"4.3.2","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He has much worthy blame laid upon him for shaking\noff so good a wife and so sweet a lady.","sourceLine":"4.3.5","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Especially he hath incurred the everlasting\ndispleasure of the king, who had even tuned his\nbounty to sing happiness to him. I will tell you a\nthing, but you shall let it dwell darkly with you.","sourceLine":"4.3.7","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"When you have spoken it, 'tis dead, and I am the\ngrave of it.","sourceLine":"4.3.11","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He hath perverted a young gentlewoman here in\nFlorence, of a most chaste renown; and this night he\nfleshes his will in the spoil of her honour: he hath\ngiven her his monumental ring, and thinks himself\nmade in the unchaste composition.","sourceLine":"4.3.13","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now, God delay our rebellion! as we are ourselves,\nwhat things are we!","sourceLine":"4.3.18","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Merely our own traitors. And as in the common course\nof all treasons, we still see them reveal\nthemselves, till they attain to their abhorred ends,\nso he that in this action contrives against his own\nnobility, in his proper stream o'erflows himself.","sourceLine":"4.3.20","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Is it not meant damnable in us, to be trumpeters of\nour unlawful intents? We shall not then have his\ncompany to-night?","sourceLine":"4.3.25","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Not till after midnight; for he is dieted to his hour.","sourceLine":"4.3.28","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That approaches apace; I would gladly have him see\nhis company anatomized, that he might take a measure\nof his own judgments, wherein so curiously he had\nset this counterfeit.","sourceLine":"4.3.29","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"We will not meddle with him till he come; for his\npresence must be the whip of the other.","sourceLine":"4.3.33","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"In the mean time, what hear you of these wars?","sourceLine":"4.3.35","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I hear there is an overture of peace.","sourceLine":"4.3.36","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, I assure you, a peace concluded.","sourceLine":"4.3.37","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What will Count Rousillon do then? will he travel\nhigher, or return again into France?","sourceLine":"4.3.38","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I perceive, by this demand, you are not altogether\nof his council.","sourceLine":"4.3.40","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let it be forbid, sir; so should I be a great deal\nof his act.","sourceLine":"4.3.42","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sir, his wife some two months since fled from his\nhouse: her pretence is a pilgrimage to Saint Jaques\nle Grand; which holy undertaking with most austere\nsanctimony she accomplished; and, there residing the\ntenderness of her nature became as a prey to her\ngrief; in fine, made a groan of her last breath, and\nnow she sings in heaven.","sourceLine":"4.3.44","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How is this justified?","sourceLine":"4.3.51","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The stronger part of it by her own letters, which\nmakes her story true, even to the point of her\ndeath: her death itself, which could not be her\noffice to say is come, was faithfully confirmed by\nthe rector of the place.","sourceLine":"4.3.52","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hath the count all this intelligence?","sourceLine":"4.3.57","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, and the particular confirmations, point from\npoint, so to the full arming of the verity.","sourceLine":"4.3.58","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I am heartily sorry that he'll be glad of this.","sourceLine":"4.3.60","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How mightily sometimes we make us comforts of our losses!","sourceLine":"4.3.61","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And how mightily some other times we drown our gain\nin tears! The great dignity that his valour hath\nhere acquired for him shall at home be encountered\nwith a shame as ample.","sourceLine":"4.3.62","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The web of our life is of a mingled yarn, good and\nill together: our virtues would be proud, if our\nfaults whipped them not; and our crimes would\ndespair, if they were not cherished by our virtues.","sourceLine":"4.3.66","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:28"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter a Messenger","id":"allswell:4.3:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How now! where's your master?","sourceLine":"4.3.70","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He met the duke in the street, sir, of whom he hath\ntaken a solemn leave: his lordship will next\nmorning for France. The duke hath offered him\nletters of commendations to the king.","sourceLine":"4.3.71","speaker":"Servant","id":"allswell:4.3:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"They shall be no more than needful there, if they\nwere more than they can commend.","sourceLine":"4.3.75","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"They cannot be too sweet for the king's tartness.\nHere's his lordship now.","sourceLine":"4.3.77","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:33"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter BERTRAM","id":"allswell:4.3:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How now, my lord! is't not after midnight?","sourceLine":"4.3.79","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I have to-night dispatched sixteen businesses, a\nmonth's length a-piece, by an abstract of success:\nI have congied with the duke, done my adieu with his\nnearest; buried a wife, mourned for her; writ to my\nlady mother I am returning; entertained my convoy;\nand between these main parcels of dispatch effected\nmany nicer needs; the last was the greatest, but\nthat I have not ended yet.","sourceLine":"4.3.80","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:4.3:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If the business be of any difficulty, and this\nmorning your departure hence, it requires haste of\nyour lordship.","sourceLine":"4.3.88","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I mean, the business is not ended, as fearing to\nhear of it hereafter. But shall we have this\ndialogue between the fool and the soldier? Come,\nbring forth this counterfeit module, he has deceived\nme, like a double-meaning prophesier.","sourceLine":"4.3.91","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:4.3:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Bring him forth: has sat i' the stocks all night,\npoor gallant knave.","sourceLine":"4.3.96","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:39"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No matter: his heels have deserved it, in usurping\nhis spurs so long. How does he carry himself?","sourceLine":"4.3.98","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:4.3:40"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I have told your lordship already, the stocks carry\nhim. But to answer you as you would be understood;\nhe weeps like a wench that had shed her milk: he\nhath confessed himself to Morgan, whom he supposes\nto be a friar, from the time of his remembrance to\nthis very instant disaster of his setting i' the\nstocks: and what think you he hath confessed?","sourceLine":"4.3.100","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:41"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nothing of me, has a'?","sourceLine":"4.3.107","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:4.3:42"},{"kind":"speech","text":"His confession is taken, and it shall be read to his\nface: if your lordship be in't, as I believe you\nare, you must have the patience to hear it.","sourceLine":"4.3.108","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:43"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter PAROLLES guarded, and First Soldier","id":"allswell:4.3:44"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A plague upon him! muffled! he can say nothing of\nme: hush, hush!","sourceLine":"4.3.111","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:4.3:45"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hoodman comes! Portotartarosa","sourceLine":"4.3.113","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:46"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He calls for the tortures: what will you say\nwithout 'em?","sourceLine":"4.3.114","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"allswell:4.3:47"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I will confess what I know without constraint: if\nye pinch me like a pasty, I can say no more.","sourceLine":"4.3.116","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:4.3:48"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Bosko chimurcho.","sourceLine":"4.3.118","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"allswell:4.3:49"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Boblibindo chicurmurco.","sourceLine":"4.3.119","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:50"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You are a merciful general. Our general bids you\nanswer to what I shall ask you out of a note.","sourceLine":"4.3.120","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"allswell:4.3:51"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And truly, as I hope to live.","sourceLine":"4.3.122","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:4.3:52"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Reads] 'First demand of him how many horse the\nduke is strong.' What say you to that?","sourceLine":"4.3.123","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"allswell:4.3:53"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Five or six thousand; but very weak and\nunserviceable: the troops are all scattered, and\nthe commanders very poor rogues, upon my reputation\nand credit and as I hope to live.","sourceLine":"4.3.125","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:4.3:54"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Shall I set down your answer so?","sourceLine":"4.3.129","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"allswell:4.3:55"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Do: I'll take the sacrament on't, how and which way you will.","sourceLine":"4.3.130","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:4.3:56"},{"kind":"speech","text":"All's one to him. What a past-saving slave is this!","sourceLine":"4.3.131","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:4.3:57"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You're deceived, my lord: this is Monsieur\nParolles, the gallant militarist,--that was his own\nphrase,--that had the whole theoric of war in the\nknot of his scarf, and the practise in the chape of\nhis dagger.","sourceLine":"4.3.132","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:58"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I will never trust a man again for keeping his sword\nclean. nor believe he can have every thing in him\nby wearing his apparel neatly.","sourceLine":"4.3.137","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:59"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well, that's set down.","sourceLine":"4.3.140","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"allswell:4.3:60"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Five or six thousand horse, I said,-- I will say\ntrue,--or thereabouts, set down, for I'll speak truth.","sourceLine":"4.3.141","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:4.3:61"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He's very near the truth in this.","sourceLine":"4.3.143","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:62"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But I con him no thanks for't, in the nature he\ndelivers it.","sourceLine":"4.3.144","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:4.3:63"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Poor rogues, I pray you, say.","sourceLine":"4.3.146","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:4.3:64"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well, that's set down.","sourceLine":"4.3.147","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"allswell:4.3:65"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I humbly thank you, sir: a truth's a truth, the\nrogues are marvellous poor.","sourceLine":"4.3.148","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:4.3:66"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Reads] 'Demand of him, of what strength they are\na-foot.' What say you to that?","sourceLine":"4.3.150","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"allswell:4.3:67"},{"kind":"speech","text":"By my troth, sir, if I were to live this present\nhour, I will tell true. Let me see: Spurio, a\nhundred and fifty; Sebastian, so many; Corambus, so\nmany; Jaques, so many; Guiltian, Cosmo, Lodowick,\nand Gratii, two hundred and fifty each; mine own\ncompany, Chitopher, Vaumond, Bentii, two hundred and\nfifty each: so that the muster-file, rotten and\nsound, upon my life, amounts not to fifteen thousand\npoll; half of the which dare not shake snow from off\ntheir cassocks, lest they shake themselves to pieces.","sourceLine":"4.3.152","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:4.3:68"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What shall be done to him?","sourceLine":"4.3.162","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:4.3:69"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nothing, but let him have thanks. Demand of him my\ncondition, and what credit I have with the duke.","sourceLine":"4.3.163","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:70"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well, that's set down.","sourceLine":"4.3.165","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"allswell:4.3:71"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Reads","id":"allswell:4.3:72"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'You shall demand of him, whether one Captain Dumain\nbe i' the camp, a Frenchman; what his reputation is\nwith the duke; what his valour, honesty, and\nexpertness in wars; or whether he thinks it were not\npossible, with well-weighing sums of gold, to\ncorrupt him to revolt.' What say you to this? what\ndo you know of it?","sourceLine":"4.3.166","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"allswell:4.3:73"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I beseech you, let me answer to the particular of\nthe inter'gatories: demand them singly.","sourceLine":"4.3.173","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:4.3:74"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Do you know this Captain Dumain?","sourceLine":"4.3.175","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"allswell:4.3:75"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I know him: a' was a botcher's 'prentice in Paris,\nfrom whence he was whipped for getting the shrieve's\nfool with child,--a dumb innocent, that could not\nsay him nay.","sourceLine":"4.3.176","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:4.3:76"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, by your leave, hold your hands; though I know\nhis brains are forfeit to the next tile that falls.","sourceLine":"4.3.180","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:4.3:77"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well, is this captain in the duke of Florence's camp?","sourceLine":"4.3.182","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"allswell:4.3:78"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Upon my knowledge, he is, and lousy.","sourceLine":"4.3.183","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:4.3:79"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay look not so upon me; we shall hear of your\nlordship anon.","sourceLine":"4.3.184","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:80"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What is his reputation with the duke?","sourceLine":"4.3.186","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"allswell:4.3:81"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The duke knows him for no other but a poor officer\nof mine; and writ to me this other day to turn him\nout o' the band: I think I have his letter in my pocket.","sourceLine":"4.3.187","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:4.3:82"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Marry, we'll search.","sourceLine":"4.3.190","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"allswell:4.3:83"},{"kind":"speech","text":"In good sadness, I do not know; either it is there,\nor it is upon a file with the duke's other letters\nin my tent.","sourceLine":"4.3.191","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:4.3:84"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Here 'tis; here's a paper: shall I read it to you?","sourceLine":"4.3.194","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"allswell:4.3:85"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I do not know if it be it or no.","sourceLine":"4.3.195","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:4.3:86"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Our interpreter does it well.","sourceLine":"4.3.196","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:4.3:87"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Excellently.","sourceLine":"4.3.197","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:88"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Reads] 'Dian, the count's a fool, and full of gold,'--","sourceLine":"4.3.198","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"allswell:4.3:89"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That is not the duke's letter, sir; that is an\nadvertisement to a proper maid in Florence, one\nDiana, to take heed of the allurement of one Count\nRousillon, a foolish idle boy, but for all that very\nruttish: I pray you, sir, put it up again.","sourceLine":"4.3.199","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:4.3:90"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, I'll read it first, by your favour.","sourceLine":"4.3.204","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"allswell:4.3:91"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My meaning in't, I protest, was very honest in the\nbehalf of the maid; for I knew the young count to be\na dangerous and lascivious boy, who is a whale to\nvirginity and devours up all the fry it finds.","sourceLine":"4.3.205","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:4.3:92"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Damnable both-sides rogue!","sourceLine":"4.3.209","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:4.3:93"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Reads] 'When he swears oaths, bid him drop gold, and take it;\nAfter he scores, he never pays the score:\nHalf won is match well made; match, and well make it;\nHe ne'er pays after-debts, take it before;\nAnd say a soldier, Dian, told thee this,\nMen are to mell with, boys are not to kiss:\nFor count of this, the count's a fool, I know it,\nWho pays before, but not when he does owe it.\nThine, as he vowed to thee in thine ear,\nPAROLLES.'","sourceLine":"4.3.210","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"allswell:4.3:94"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He shall be whipped through the army with this rhyme\nin's forehead.","sourceLine":"4.3.220","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:4.3:95"},{"kind":"speech","text":"This is your devoted friend, sir, the manifold\nlinguist and the armipotent soldier.","sourceLine":"4.3.222","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:96"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I could endure any thing before but a cat, and now\nhe's a cat to me.","sourceLine":"4.3.224","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:4.3:97"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I perceive, sir, by the general's looks, we shall be\nfain to hang you.","sourceLine":"4.3.226","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"allswell:4.3:98"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My life, sir, in any case: not that I am afraid to\ndie; but that, my offences being many, I would\nrepent out the remainder of nature: let me live,\nsir, in a dungeon, i' the stocks, or any where, so I may live.","sourceLine":"4.3.228","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:4.3:99"},{"kind":"speech","text":"We'll see what may be done, so you confess freely;\ntherefore, once more to this Captain Dumain: you\nhave answered to his reputation with the duke and to\nhis valour: what is his honesty?","sourceLine":"4.3.232","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"allswell:4.3:100"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He will steal, sir, an egg out of a cloister: for\nrapes and ravishments he parallels Nessus: he\nprofesses not keeping of oaths; in breaking 'em he\nis stronger than Hercules: he will lie, sir, with\nsuch volubility, that you would think truth were a\nfool: drunkenness is his best virtue, for he will\nbe swine-drunk; and in his sleep he does little\nharm, save to his bed-clothes about him; but they\nknow his conditions and lay him in straw. I have but\nlittle more to say, sir, of his honesty: he has\nevery thing that an honest man should not have; what\nan honest man should have, he has nothing.","sourceLine":"4.3.236","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:4.3:101"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I begin to love him for this.","sourceLine":"4.3.248","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:102"},{"kind":"speech","text":"For this description of thine honesty? A pox upon\nhim for me, he's more and more a cat.","sourceLine":"4.3.249","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:4.3:103"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What say you to his expertness in war?","sourceLine":"4.3.251","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"allswell:4.3:104"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Faith, sir, he has led the drum before the English\ntragedians; to belie him, I will not, and more of\nhis soldiership I know not; except, in that country\nhe had the honour to be the officer at a place there\ncalled Mile-end, to instruct for the doubling of\nfiles: I would do the man what honour I can, but of\nthis I am not certain.","sourceLine":"4.3.252","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:4.3:105"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He hath out-villained villany so far, that the\nrarity redeems him.","sourceLine":"4.3.259","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:106"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A pox on him, he's a cat still.","sourceLine":"4.3.261","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:4.3:107"},{"kind":"speech","text":"His qualities being at this poor price, I need not\nto ask you if gold will corrupt him to revolt.","sourceLine":"4.3.262","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"allswell:4.3:108"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sir, for a quart d'ecu he will sell the fee-simple\nof his salvation, the inheritance of it; and cut the\nentail from all remainders, and a perpetual\nsuccession for it perpetually.","sourceLine":"4.3.264","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:4.3:109"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What's his brother, the other Captain Dumain?","sourceLine":"4.3.268","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"allswell:4.3:110"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why does be ask him of me?","sourceLine":"4.3.269","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:111"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What's he?","sourceLine":"4.3.270","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"allswell:4.3:112"},{"kind":"speech","text":"E'en a crow o' the same nest; not altogether so\ngreat as the first in goodness, but greater a great\ndeal in evil: he excels his brother for a coward,\nyet his brother is reputed one of the best that is:\nin a retreat he outruns any lackey; marry, in coming\non he has the cramp.","sourceLine":"4.3.271","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:4.3:113"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If your life be saved, will you undertake to betray\nthe Florentine?","sourceLine":"4.3.277","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"allswell:4.3:114"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, and the captain of his horse, Count Rousillon.","sourceLine":"4.3.279","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:4.3:115"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I'll whisper with the general, and know his pleasure.","sourceLine":"4.3.280","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"allswell:4.3:116"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Aside] I'll no more drumming; a plague of all\ndrums! Only to seem to deserve well, and to\nbeguile the supposition of that lascivious young boy\nthe count, have I run into this danger. Yet who\nwould have suspected an ambush where I was taken?","sourceLine":"4.3.281","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:4.3:117"},{"kind":"speech","text":"There is no remedy, sir, but you must die: the\ngeneral says, you that have so traitorously\ndiscovered the secrets of your army and made such\npestiferous reports of men very nobly held, can\nserve the world for no honest use; therefore you\nmust die. Come, headsman, off with his head.","sourceLine":"4.3.286","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"allswell:4.3:118"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O Lord, sir, let me live, or let me see my death!","sourceLine":"4.3.292","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:4.3:119"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That shall you, and take your leave of all your friends.","sourceLine":"4.3.293","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:120"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Unblinding him","id":"allswell:4.3:121"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So, look about you: know you any here?","sourceLine":"4.3.294","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:122"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Good morrow, noble captain.","sourceLine":"4.3.295","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:4.3:123"},{"kind":"speech","text":"God bless you, Captain Parolles.","sourceLine":"4.3.296","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:124"},{"kind":"speech","text":"God save you, noble captain.","sourceLine":"4.3.297","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:125"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Captain, what greeting will you to my Lord Lafeu?\nI am for France.","sourceLine":"4.3.298","speaker":"Second Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:126"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Good captain, will you give me a copy of the sonnet\nyou writ to Diana in behalf of the Count Rousillon?\nan I were not a very coward, I'ld compel it of you:\nbut fare you well.","sourceLine":"4.3.300","speaker":"First Lord","id":"allswell:4.3:127"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt BERTRAM and Lords","id":"allswell:4.3:128"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You are undone, captain, all but your scarf; that\nhas a knot on't yet","sourceLine":"4.3.304","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"allswell:4.3:129"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Who cannot be crushed with a plot?","sourceLine":"4.3.306","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:4.3:130"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If you could find out a country where but women were\nthat had received so much shame, you might begin an\nimpudent nation. Fare ye well, sir; I am for France\ntoo: we shall speak of you there.","sourceLine":"4.3.307","speaker":"First Soldier","id":"allswell:4.3:131"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit with Soldiers","id":"allswell:4.3:132"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yet am I thankful: if my heart were great,\n'Twould burst at this. Captain I'll be no more;\nBut I will eat and drink, and sleep as soft\nAs captain shall: simply the thing I am\nShall make me live. Who knows himself a braggart,\nLet him fear this, for it will come to pass\nthat every braggart shall be found an ass.\nRust, sword? cool, blushes! and, Parolles, live\nSafest in shame! being fool'd, by foolery thrive!\nThere's place and means for every man alive.\nI'll after them.","sourceLine":"4.3.311","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:4.3:133"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"allswell:4.3:134"}],"scene":3},{"id":"4.4","label":"Act 4 · SCENE IV. Florence. The Widow's house.","act":4,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/allswell/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter HELENA, Widow, and DIANA","id":"allswell:4.4:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That you may well perceive I have not wrong'd you,\nOne of the greatest in the Christian world\nShall be my surety; 'fore whose throne 'tis needful,\nEre I can perfect mine intents, to kneel:\nTime was, I did him a desired office,\nDear almost as his life; which gratitude\nThrough flinty Tartar's bosom would peep forth,\nAnd answer, thanks: I duly am inform'd\nHis grace is at Marseilles; to which place\nWe have convenient convoy. You must know\nI am supposed dead: the army breaking,\nMy husband hies him home; where, heaven aiding,\nAnd by the leave of my good lord the king,\nWe'll be before our welcome.","sourceLine":"4.4.1","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:4.4:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Gentle madam,\nYou never had a servant to whose trust\nYour business was more welcome.","sourceLine":"4.4.15","speaker":"Widow","id":"allswell:4.4:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nor you, mistress,\nEver a friend whose thoughts more truly labour\nTo recompense your love: doubt not but heaven\nHath brought me up to be your daughter's dower,\nAs it hath fated her to be my motive\nAnd helper to a husband. But, O strange men!\nThat can such sweet use make of what they hate,\nWhen saucy trusting of the cozen'd thoughts\nDefiles the pitchy night: so lust doth play\nWith what it loathes for that which is away.\nBut more of this hereafter. You, Diana,\nUnder my poor instructions yet must suffer\nSomething in my behalf.","sourceLine":"4.4.18","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:4.4:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let death and honesty\nGo with your impositions, I am yours\nUpon your will to suffer.","sourceLine":"4.4.31","speaker":"DIANA","id":"allswell:4.4:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yet, I pray you:\nBut with the word the time will bring on summer,\nWhen briers shall have leaves as well as thorns,\nAnd be as sweet as sharp. We must away;\nOur wagon is prepared, and time revives us:\nAll's well that ends well; still the fine's the crown;\nWhate'er the course, the end is the renown.","sourceLine":"4.4.34","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:4.4:6"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"allswell:4.4:7"}],"scene":4},{"id":"4.5","label":"Act 4 · SCENE V. Rousillon. The COUNT's palace.","act":4,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/allswell/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter COUNTESS, LAFEU, and Clown","id":"allswell:4.5:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No, no, no, your son was misled with a snipt-taffeta\nfellow there, whose villanous saffron would have\nmade all the unbaked and doughy youth of a nation in\nhis colour: your daughter-in-law had been alive at\nthis hour, and your son here at home, more advanced\nby the king than by that red-tailed humble-bee I speak of.","sourceLine":"4.5.1","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:4.5:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I would I had not known him; it was the death of the\nmost virtuous gentlewoman that ever nature had\npraise for creating. If she had partaken of my\nflesh, and cost me the dearest groans of a mother, I\ncould not have owed her a more rooted love.","sourceLine":"4.5.7","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:4.5:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'Twas a good lady, 'twas a good lady: we may pick a\nthousand salads ere we light on such another herb.","sourceLine":"4.5.12","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:4.5:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Indeed, sir, she was the sweet marjoram of the\nsalad, or rather, the herb of grace.","sourceLine":"4.5.14","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:4.5:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"They are not herbs, you knave; they are nose-herbs.","sourceLine":"4.5.16","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:4.5:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I am no great Nebuchadnezzar, sir; I have not much\nskill in grass.","sourceLine":"4.5.17","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:4.5:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Whether dost thou profess thyself, a knave or a fool?","sourceLine":"4.5.19","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:4.5:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A fool, sir, at a woman's service, and a knave at a man's.","sourceLine":"4.5.20","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:4.5:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Your distinction?","sourceLine":"4.5.21","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:4.5:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I would cozen the man of his wife and do his service.","sourceLine":"4.5.22","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:4.5:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So you were a knave at his service, indeed.","sourceLine":"4.5.23","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:4.5:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And I would give his wife my bauble, sir, to do her service.","sourceLine":"4.5.24","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:4.5:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I will subscribe for thee, thou art both knave and fool.","sourceLine":"4.5.25","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:4.5:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"At your service.","sourceLine":"4.5.26","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:4.5:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No, no, no.","sourceLine":"4.5.27","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:4.5:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why, sir, if I cannot serve you, I can serve as\ngreat a prince as you are.","sourceLine":"4.5.28","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:4.5:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Who's that? a Frenchman?","sourceLine":"4.5.30","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:4.5:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Faith, sir, a' has an English name; but his fisnomy\nis more hotter in France than there.","sourceLine":"4.5.31","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:4.5:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What prince is that?","sourceLine":"4.5.33","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:4.5:20"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The black prince, sir; alias, the prince of\ndarkness; alias, the devil.","sourceLine":"4.5.34","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:4.5:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hold thee, there's my purse: I give thee not this\nto suggest thee from thy master thou talkest of;\nserve him still.","sourceLine":"4.5.36","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:4.5:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I am a woodland fellow, sir, that always loved a\ngreat fire; and the master I speak of ever keeps a\ngood fire. But, sure, he is the prince of the\nworld; let his nobility remain in's court. I am for\nthe house with the narrow gate, which I take to be\ntoo little for pomp to enter: some that humble\nthemselves may; but the many will be too chill and\ntender, and they'll be for the flowery way that\nleads to the broad gate and the great fire.","sourceLine":"4.5.39","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:4.5:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Go thy ways, I begin to be aweary of thee; and I\ntell thee so before, because I would not fall out\nwith thee. Go thy ways: let my horses be well\nlooked to, without any tricks.","sourceLine":"4.5.48","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:4.5:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If I put any tricks upon 'em, sir, they shall be\njades' tricks; which are their own right by the law of nature.","sourceLine":"4.5.52","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:4.5:25"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"allswell:4.5:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A shrewd knave and an unhappy.","sourceLine":"4.5.54","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:4.5:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So he is. My lord that's gone made himself much\nsport out of him: by his authority he remains here,\nwhich he thinks is a patent for his sauciness; and,\nindeed, he has no pace, but runs where he will.","sourceLine":"4.5.55","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:4.5:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I like him well; 'tis not amiss. And I was about to\ntell you, since I heard of the good lady's death and\nthat my lord your son was upon his return home, I\nmoved the king my master to speak in the behalf of\nmy daughter; which, in the minority of them both,\nhis majesty, out of a self-gracious remembrance, did\nfirst propose: his highness hath promised me to do\nit: and, to stop up the displeasure he hath\nconceived against your son, there is no fitter\nmatter. How does your ladyship like it?","sourceLine":"4.5.59","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:4.5:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"With very much content, my lord; and I wish it\nhappily effected.","sourceLine":"4.5.69","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:4.5:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"His highness comes post from Marseilles, of as able\nbody as when he numbered thirty: he will be here\nto-morrow, or I am deceived by him that in such\nintelligence hath seldom failed.","sourceLine":"4.5.71","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:4.5:31"},{"kind":"speech","text":"It rejoices me, that I hope I shall see him ere I\ndie. I have letters that my son will be here\nto-night: I shall beseech your lordship to remain\nwith me till they meet together.","sourceLine":"4.5.75","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:4.5:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Madam, I was thinking with what manners I might\nsafely be admitted.","sourceLine":"4.5.79","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:4.5:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You need but plead your honourable privilege.","sourceLine":"4.5.81","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:4.5:34"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Lady, of that I have made a bold charter; but I\nthank my God it holds yet.","sourceLine":"4.5.82","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:4.5:35"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Re-enter Clown","id":"allswell:4.5:36"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O madam, yonder's my lord your son with a patch of\nvelvet on's face: whether there be a scar under't\nor no, the velvet knows; but 'tis a goodly patch of\nvelvet: his left cheek is a cheek of two pile and a\nhalf, but his right cheek is worn bare.","sourceLine":"4.5.84","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:4.5:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"A scar nobly got, or a noble scar, is a good livery\nof honour; so belike is that.","sourceLine":"4.5.89","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:4.5:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But it is your carbonadoed face.","sourceLine":"4.5.91","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:4.5:39"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let us go see your son, I pray you: I long to talk\nwith the young noble soldier.","sourceLine":"4.5.92","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:4.5:40"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Faith there's a dozen of 'em, with delicate fine\nhats and most courteous feathers, which bow the head\nand nod at every man.","sourceLine":"4.5.94","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:4.5:41"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"allswell:4.5:42"}],"scene":5},{"id":"5.1","label":"Act 5 · SCENE I. Marseilles. A street.","act":5,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/allswell/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter HELENA, Widow, and DIANA, with two Attendants","id":"allswell:5.1:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But this exceeding posting day and night\nMust wear your spirits low; we cannot help it:\nBut since you have made the days and nights as one,\nTo wear your gentle limbs in my affairs,\nBe bold you do so grow in my requital\nAs nothing can unroot you. In happy time;","sourceLine":"5.1.1","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:5.1:2"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter a Gentleman","id":"allswell:5.1:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"This man may help me to his majesty's ear,\nIf he would spend his power. God save you, sir.","sourceLine":"5.1.7","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:5.1:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And you.","sourceLine":"5.1.9","speaker":"Gentleman","id":"allswell:5.1:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sir, I have seen you in the court of France.","sourceLine":"5.1.10","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:5.1:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I have been sometimes there.","sourceLine":"5.1.11","speaker":"Gentleman","id":"allswell:5.1:7"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I do presume, sir, that you are not fallen\nFrom the report that goes upon your goodness;\nAn therefore, goaded with most sharp occasions,\nWhich lay nice manners by, I put you to\nThe use of your own virtues, for the which\nI shall continue thankful.","sourceLine":"5.1.12","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:5.1:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What's your will?","sourceLine":"5.1.18","speaker":"Gentleman","id":"allswell:5.1:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"That it will please you\nTo give this poor petition to the king,\nAnd aid me with that store of power you have\nTo come into his presence.","sourceLine":"5.1.19","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:5.1:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The king's not here.","sourceLine":"5.1.23","speaker":"Gentleman","id":"allswell:5.1:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Not here, sir!","sourceLine":"5.1.24","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:5.1:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Not, indeed:\nHe hence removed last night and with more haste\nThan is his use.","sourceLine":"5.1.25","speaker":"Gentleman","id":"allswell:5.1:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Lord, how we lose our pains!","sourceLine":"5.1.28","speaker":"Widow","id":"allswell:5.1:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"ALL'S WELL THAT ENDS WELL yet,\nThough time seem so adverse and means unfit.\nI do beseech you, whither is he gone?","sourceLine":"5.1.29","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:5.1:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Marry, as I take it, to Rousillon;\nWhither I am going.","sourceLine":"5.1.32","speaker":"Gentleman","id":"allswell:5.1:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I do beseech you, sir,\nSince you are like to see the king before me,\nCommend the paper to his gracious hand,\nWhich I presume shall render you no blame\nBut rather make you thank your pains for it.\nI will come after you with what good speed\nOur means will make us means.","sourceLine":"5.1.34","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:5.1:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"This I'll do for you.","sourceLine":"5.1.41","speaker":"Gentleman","id":"allswell:5.1:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And you shall find yourself to be well thank'd,\nWhate'er falls more. We must to horse again.\nGo, go, provide.","sourceLine":"5.1.42","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:5.1:19"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"allswell:5.1:20"}],"scene":1},{"id":"5.2","label":"Act 5 · SCENE II. Rousillon. Before the COUNT's palace.","act":5,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/allswell/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Clown, and PAROLLES, following","id":"allswell:5.2:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Good Monsieur Lavache, give my Lord Lafeu this\nletter: I have ere now, sir, been better known to\nyou, when I have held familiarity with fresher\nclothes; but I am now, sir, muddied in fortune's\nmood, and smell somewhat strong of her strong\ndispleasure.","sourceLine":"5.2.1","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:5.2:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Truly, fortune's displeasure is but sluttish, if it\nsmell so strongly as thou speakest of: I will\nhenceforth eat no fish of fortune's buttering.\nPrithee, allow the wind.","sourceLine":"5.2.7","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:5.2:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Nay, you need not to stop your nose, sir; I spake\nbut by a metaphor.","sourceLine":"5.2.11","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:5.2:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Indeed, sir, if your metaphor stink, I will stop my\nnose; or against any man's metaphor. Prithee, get\nthee further.","sourceLine":"5.2.13","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:5.2:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Pray you, sir, deliver me this paper.","sourceLine":"5.2.16","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:5.2:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Foh! prithee, stand away: a paper from fortune's\nclose-stool to give to a nobleman! Look, here he\ncomes himself.","sourceLine":"5.2.17","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:5.2:7"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter LAFEU","id":"allswell:5.2:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Here is a purr of fortune's, sir, or of fortune's\ncat,--but not a musk-cat,--that has fallen into the\nunclean fishpond of her displeasure, and, as he\nsays, is muddied withal: pray you, sir, use the\ncarp as you may; for he looks like a poor, decayed,\ningenious, foolish, rascally knave. I do pity his\ndistress in my similes of comfort and leave him to\nyour lordship.","sourceLine":"5.2.20","speaker":"Clown","id":"allswell:5.2:9"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"allswell:5.2:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord, I am a man whom fortune hath cruelly\nscratched.","sourceLine":"5.2.28","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:5.2:11"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And what would you have me to do? 'Tis too late to\npare her nails now. Wherein have you played the\nknave with fortune, that she should scratch you, who\nof herself is a good lady and would not have knaves\nthrive long under her? There's a quart d'ecu for\nyou: let the justices make you and fortune friends:\nI am for other business.","sourceLine":"5.2.30","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:5.2:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I beseech your honour to hear me one single word.","sourceLine":"5.2.37","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:5.2:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You beg a single penny more: come, you shall ha't;\nsave your word.","sourceLine":"5.2.38","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:5.2:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My name, my good lord, is Parolles.","sourceLine":"5.2.40","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:5.2:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You beg more than 'word,' then. Cox my passion!\ngive me your hand. How does your drum?","sourceLine":"5.2.41","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:5.2:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O my good lord, you were the first that found me!","sourceLine":"5.2.43","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:5.2:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Was I, in sooth? and I was the first that lost thee.","sourceLine":"5.2.44","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:5.2:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"It lies in you, my lord, to bring me in some grace,\nfor you did bring me out.","sourceLine":"5.2.45","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:5.2:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Out upon thee, knave! dost thou put upon me at once\nboth the office of God and the devil? One brings\nthee in grace and the other brings thee out.","sourceLine":"5.2.47","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:5.2:20"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Trumpets sound","id":"allswell:5.2:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The king's coming; I know by his trumpets. Sirrah,\ninquire further after me; I had talk of you last\nnight: though you are a fool and a knave, you shall\neat; go to, follow.","sourceLine":"5.2.50","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:5.2:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I praise God for you.","sourceLine":"5.2.54","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:5.2:23"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"allswell:5.2:24"}],"scene":2},{"id":"5.3","label":"Act 5 · SCENE III. Rousillon. The COUNT's palace.","act":5,"sourceUrl":"https://shakespeare.mit.edu/allswell/full.html","blocks":[{"kind":"stage","text":"Flourish. Enter KING, COUNTESS, LAFEU, the two French Lords, with Attendants","id":"allswell:5.3:1"},{"kind":"speech","text":"We lost a jewel of her; and our esteem\nWas made much poorer by it: but your son,\nAs mad in folly, lack'd the sense to know\nHer estimation home.","sourceLine":"5.3.1","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:5.3:2"},{"kind":"speech","text":"'Tis past, my liege;\nAnd I beseech your majesty to make it\nNatural rebellion, done i' the blaze of youth;\nWhen oil and fire, too strong for reason's force,\nO'erbears it and burns on.","sourceLine":"5.3.5","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:5.3:3"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My honour'd lady,\nI have forgiven and forgotten all;\nThough my revenges were high bent upon him,\nAnd watch'd the time to shoot.","sourceLine":"5.3.10","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:5.3:4"},{"kind":"speech","text":"This I must say,\nBut first I beg my pardon, the young lord\nDid to his majesty, his mother and his lady\nOffence of mighty note; but to himself\nThe greatest wrong of all. He lost a wife\nWhose beauty did astonish the survey\nOf richest eyes, whose words all ears took captive,\nWhose dear perfection hearts that scorn'd to serve\nHumbly call'd mistress.","sourceLine":"5.3.14","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:5.3:5"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Praising what is lost\nMakes the remembrance dear. Well, call him hither;\nWe are reconciled, and the first view shall kill\nAll repetition: let him not ask our pardon;\nThe nature of his great offence is dead,\nAnd deeper than oblivion we do bury\nThe incensing relics of it: let him approach,\nA stranger, no offender; and inform him\nSo 'tis our will he should.","sourceLine":"5.3.23","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:5.3:6"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I shall, my liege.","sourceLine":"5.3.32","speaker":"Gentleman","id":"allswell:5.3:7"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit","id":"allswell:5.3:8"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What says he to your daughter? have you spoke?","sourceLine":"5.3.33","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:5.3:9"},{"kind":"speech","text":"All that he is hath reference to your highness.","sourceLine":"5.3.34","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:5.3:10"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Then shall we have a match. I have letters sent me\nThat set him high in fame.","sourceLine":"5.3.35","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:5.3:11"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter BERTRAM","id":"allswell:5.3:12"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He looks well on't.","sourceLine":"5.3.37","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:5.3:13"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I am not a day of season,\nFor thou mayst see a sunshine and a hail\nIn me at once: but to the brightest beams\nDistracted clouds give way; so stand thou forth;\nThe time is fair again.","sourceLine":"5.3.38","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:5.3:14"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My high-repented blames,\nDear sovereign, pardon to me.","sourceLine":"5.3.43","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:5.3:15"},{"kind":"speech","text":"All is whole;\nNot one word more of the consumed time.\nLet's take the instant by the forward top;\nFor we are old, and on our quick'st decrees\nThe inaudible and noiseless foot of Time\nSteals ere we can effect them. You remember\nThe daughter of this lord?","sourceLine":"5.3.45","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:5.3:16"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Admiringly, my liege, at first\nI stuck my choice upon her, ere my heart\nDurst make too bold a herald of my tongue\nWhere the impression of mine eye infixing,\nContempt his scornful perspective did lend me,\nWhich warp'd the line of every other favour;\nScorn'd a fair colour, or express'd it stolen;\nExtended or contracted all proportions\nTo a most hideous object: thence it came\nThat she whom all men praised and whom myself,\nSince I have lost, have loved, was in mine eye\nThe dust that did offend it.","sourceLine":"5.3.52","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:5.3:17"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Well excused:\nThat thou didst love her, strikes some scores away\nFrom the great compt: but love that comes too late,\nLike a remorseful pardon slowly carried,\nTo the great sender turns a sour offence,\nCrying, 'That's good that's gone.' Our rash faults\nMake trivial price of serious things we have,\nNot knowing them until we know their grave:\nOft our displeasures, to ourselves unjust,\nDestroy our friends and after weep their dust\nOur own love waking cries to see what's done,\nWhile shame full late sleeps out the afternoon.\nBe this sweet Helen's knell, and now forget her.\nSend forth your amorous token for fair Maudlin:\nThe main consents are had; and here we'll stay\nTo see our widower's second marriage-day.","sourceLine":"5.3.64","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:5.3:18"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Which better than the first, O dear heaven, bless!\nOr, ere they meet, in me, O nature, cesse!","sourceLine":"5.3.80","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:5.3:19"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come on, my son, in whom my house's name\nMust be digested, give a favour from you\nTo sparkle in the spirits of my daughter,\nThat she may quickly come.","sourceLine":"5.3.82","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:5.3:20"},{"kind":"stage","text":"BERTRAM gives a ring","id":"allswell:5.3:21"},{"kind":"speech","text":"By my old beard,\nAnd every hair that's on't, Helen, that's dead,\nWas a sweet creature: such a ring as this,\nThe last that e'er I took her at court,\nI saw upon her finger.","sourceLine":"5.3.86","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:5.3:22"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Hers it was not.","sourceLine":"5.3.91","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:5.3:23"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now, pray you, let me see it; for mine eye,\nWhile I was speaking, oft was fasten'd to't.\nThis ring was mine; and, when I gave it Helen,\nI bade her, if her fortunes ever stood\nNecessitied to help, that by this token\nI would relieve her. Had you that craft, to reave\nher\nOf what should stead her most?","sourceLine":"5.3.92","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:5.3:24"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My gracious sovereign,\nHowe'er it pleases you to take it so,\nThe ring was never hers.","sourceLine":"5.3.100","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:5.3:25"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Son, on my life,\nI have seen her wear it; and she reckon'd it\nAt her life's rate.","sourceLine":"5.3.103","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:5.3:26"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I am sure I saw her wear it.","sourceLine":"5.3.106","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:5.3:27"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You are deceived, my lord; she never saw it:\nIn Florence was it from a casement thrown me,\nWrapp'd in a paper, which contain'd the name\nOf her that threw it: noble she was, and thought\nI stood engaged: but when I had subscribed\nTo mine own fortune and inform'd her fully\nI could not answer in that course of honour\nAs she had made the overture, she ceased\nIn heavy satisfaction and would never\nReceive the ring again.","sourceLine":"5.3.107","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:5.3:28"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Plutus himself,\nThat knows the tinct and multiplying medicine,\nHath not in nature's mystery more science\nThan I have in this ring: 'twas mine, 'twas Helen's,\nWhoever gave it you. Then, if you know\nThat you are well acquainted with yourself,\nConfess 'twas hers, and by what rough enforcement\nYou got it from her: she call'd the saints to surety\nThat she would never put it from her finger,\nUnless she gave it to yourself in bed,\nWhere you have never come, or sent it us\nUpon her great disaster.","sourceLine":"5.3.117","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:5.3:29"},{"kind":"speech","text":"She never saw it.","sourceLine":"5.3.129","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:5.3:30"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thou speak'st it falsely, as I love mine honour;\nAnd makest conjectural fears to come into me\nWhich I would fain shut out. If it should prove\nThat thou art so inhuman,--'twill not prove so;--\nAnd yet I know not: thou didst hate her deadly,\nAnd she is dead; which nothing, but to close\nHer eyes myself, could win me to believe,\nMore than to see this ring. Take him away.","sourceLine":"5.3.130","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:5.3:31"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Guards seize BERTRAM","id":"allswell:5.3:32"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My fore-past proofs, howe'er the matter fall,\nShall tax my fears of little vanity,\nHaving vainly fear'd too little. Away with him!\nWe'll sift this matter further.","sourceLine":"5.3.138","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:5.3:33"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If you shall prove\nThis ring was ever hers, you shall as easy\nProve that I husbanded her bed in Florence,\nWhere yet she never was.","sourceLine":"5.3.142","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:5.3:34"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit, guarded","id":"allswell:5.3:35"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I am wrapp'd in dismal thinkings.","sourceLine":"5.3.146","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:5.3:36"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter a Gentleman","id":"allswell:5.3:37"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Gracious sovereign,\nWhether I have been to blame or no, I know not:\nHere's a petition from a Florentine,\nWho hath for four or five removes come short\nTo tender it herself. I undertook it,\nVanquish'd thereto by the fair grace and speech\nOf the poor suppliant, who by this I know\nIs here attending: her business looks in her\nWith an importing visage; and she told me,\nIn a sweet verbal brief, it did concern\nYour highness with herself.","sourceLine":"5.3.147","speaker":"Gentleman","id":"allswell:5.3:38"},{"kind":"speech","text":"[Reads] Upon his many protestations to marry me\nwhen his wife was dead, I blush to say it, he won\nme. Now is the Count Rousillon a widower: his vows\nare forfeited to me, and my honour's paid to him. He\nstole from Florence, taking no leave, and I follow\nhim to his country for justice: grant it me, O\nking! in you it best lies; otherwise a seducer\nflourishes, and a poor maid is undone.\nDIANA CAPILET.","sourceLine":"5.3.158","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:5.3:39"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I will buy me a son-in-law in a fair, and toll for\nthis: I'll none of him.","sourceLine":"5.3.167","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:5.3:40"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The heavens have thought well on thee Lafeu,\nTo bring forth this discovery. Seek these suitors:\nGo speedily and bring again the count.\nI am afeard the life of Helen, lady,\nWas foully snatch'd.","sourceLine":"5.3.169","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:5.3:41"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Now, justice on the doers!","sourceLine":"5.3.174","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:5.3:42"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Re-enter BERTRAM, guarded","id":"allswell:5.3:43"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I wonder, sir, sith wives are monsters to you,\nAnd that you fly them as you swear them lordship,\nYet you desire to marry.","sourceLine":"5.3.175","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:5.3:44"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter Widow and DIANA","id":"allswell:5.3:45"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What woman's that?","sourceLine":"5.3.178","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:5.3:46"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I am, my lord, a wretched Florentine,\nDerived from the ancient Capilet:\nMy suit, as I do understand, you know,\nAnd therefore know how far I may be pitied.","sourceLine":"5.3.179","speaker":"DIANA","id":"allswell:5.3:47"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I am her mother, sir, whose age and honour\nBoth suffer under this complaint we bring,\nAnd both shall cease, without your remedy.","sourceLine":"5.3.183","speaker":"Widow","id":"allswell:5.3:48"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come hither, count; do you know these women?","sourceLine":"5.3.186","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:5.3:49"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord, I neither can nor will deny\nBut that I know them: do they charge me further?","sourceLine":"5.3.187","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:5.3:50"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Why do you look so strange upon your wife?","sourceLine":"5.3.189","speaker":"DIANA","id":"allswell:5.3:51"},{"kind":"speech","text":"She's none of mine, my lord.","sourceLine":"5.3.190","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:5.3:52"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If you shall marry,\nYou give away this hand, and that is mine;\nYou give away heaven's vows, and those are mine;\nYou give away myself, which is known mine;\nFor I by vow am so embodied yours,\nThat she which marries you must marry me,\nEither both or none.","sourceLine":"5.3.191","speaker":"DIANA","id":"allswell:5.3:53"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Your reputation comes too short for my daughter; you\nare no husband for her.","sourceLine":"5.3.198","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:5.3:54"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord, this is a fond and desperate creature,\nWhom sometime I have laugh'd with: let your highness\nLay a more noble thought upon mine honour\nThan for to think that I would sink it here.","sourceLine":"5.3.200","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:5.3:55"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sir, for my thoughts, you have them ill to friend\nTill your deeds gain them: fairer prove your honour\nThan in my thought it lies.","sourceLine":"5.3.204","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:5.3:56"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Good my lord,\nAsk him upon his oath, if he does think\nHe had not my virginity.","sourceLine":"5.3.207","speaker":"DIANA","id":"allswell:5.3:57"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What say'st thou to her?","sourceLine":"5.3.210","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:5.3:58"},{"kind":"speech","text":"She's impudent, my lord,\nAnd was a common gamester to the camp.","sourceLine":"5.3.211","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:5.3:59"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He does me wrong, my lord; if I were so,\nHe might have bought me at a common price:\nDo not believe him. O, behold this ring,\nWhose high respect and rich validity\nDid lack a parallel; yet for all that\nHe gave it to a commoner o' the camp,\nIf I be one.","sourceLine":"5.3.213","speaker":"DIANA","id":"allswell:5.3:60"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He blushes, and 'tis it:\nOf six preceding ancestors, that gem,\nConferr'd by testament to the sequent issue,\nHath it been owed and worn. This is his wife;\nThat ring's a thousand proofs.","sourceLine":"5.3.220","speaker":"COUNTESS","id":"allswell:5.3:61"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Methought you said\nYou saw one here in court could witness it.","sourceLine":"5.3.225","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:5.3:62"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I did, my lord, but loath am to produce\nSo bad an instrument: his name's Parolles.","sourceLine":"5.3.227","speaker":"DIANA","id":"allswell:5.3:63"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I saw the man to-day, if man he be.","sourceLine":"5.3.229","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:5.3:64"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Find him, and bring him hither.","sourceLine":"5.3.230","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:5.3:65"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit an Attendant","id":"allswell:5.3:66"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What of him?\nHe's quoted for a most perfidious slave,\nWith all the spots o' the world tax'd and debosh'd;\nWhose nature sickens but to speak a truth.\nAm I or that or this for what he'll utter,\nThat will speak any thing?","sourceLine":"5.3.231","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:5.3:67"},{"kind":"speech","text":"She hath that ring of yours.","sourceLine":"5.3.237","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:5.3:68"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I think she has: certain it is I liked her,\nAnd boarded her i' the wanton way of youth:\nShe knew her distance and did angle for me,\nMadding my eagerness with her restraint,\nAs all impediments in fancy's course\nAre motives of more fancy; and, in fine,\nHer infinite cunning, with her modern grace,\nSubdued me to her rate: she got the ring;\nAnd I had that which any inferior might\nAt market-price have bought.","sourceLine":"5.3.238","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:5.3:69"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I must be patient:\nYou, that have turn'd off a first so noble wife,\nMay justly diet me. I pray you yet;\nSince you lack virtue, I will lose a husband;\nSend for your ring, I will return it home,\nAnd give me mine again.","sourceLine":"5.3.248","speaker":"DIANA","id":"allswell:5.3:70"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I have it not.","sourceLine":"5.3.254","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:5.3:71"},{"kind":"speech","text":"What ring was yours, I pray you?","sourceLine":"5.3.255","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:5.3:72"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Sir, much like\nThe same upon your finger.","sourceLine":"5.3.256","speaker":"DIANA","id":"allswell:5.3:73"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Know you this ring? this ring was his of late.","sourceLine":"5.3.258","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:5.3:74"},{"kind":"speech","text":"And this was it I gave him, being abed.","sourceLine":"5.3.259","speaker":"DIANA","id":"allswell:5.3:75"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The story then goes false, you threw it him\nOut of a casement.","sourceLine":"5.3.260","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:5.3:76"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I have spoke the truth.","sourceLine":"5.3.262","speaker":"DIANA","id":"allswell:5.3:77"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Enter PAROLLES","id":"allswell:5.3:78"},{"kind":"speech","text":"My lord, I do confess the ring was hers.","sourceLine":"5.3.263","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:5.3:79"},{"kind":"speech","text":"You boggle shrewdly, every feather stars you.\nIs this the man you speak of?","sourceLine":"5.3.264","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:5.3:80"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, my lord.","sourceLine":"5.3.266","speaker":"DIANA","id":"allswell:5.3:81"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Tell me, sirrah, but tell me true, I charge you,\nNot fearing the displeasure of your master,\nWhich on your just proceeding I'll keep off,\nBy him and by this woman here what know you?","sourceLine":"5.3.267","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:5.3:82"},{"kind":"speech","text":"So please your majesty, my master hath been an\nhonourable gentleman: tricks he hath had in him,\nwhich gentlemen have.","sourceLine":"5.3.271","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:5.3:83"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Come, come, to the purpose: did he love this woman?","sourceLine":"5.3.274","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:5.3:84"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Faith, sir, he did love her; but how?","sourceLine":"5.3.275","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:5.3:85"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How, I pray you?","sourceLine":"5.3.276","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:5.3:86"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He did love her, sir, as a gentleman loves a woman.","sourceLine":"5.3.277","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:5.3:87"},{"kind":"speech","text":"How is that?","sourceLine":"5.3.278","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:5.3:88"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He loved her, sir, and loved her not.","sourceLine":"5.3.279","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:5.3:89"},{"kind":"speech","text":"As thou art a knave, and no knave. What an\nequivocal companion is this!","sourceLine":"5.3.280","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:5.3:90"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I am a poor man, and at your majesty's command.","sourceLine":"5.3.282","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:5.3:91"},{"kind":"speech","text":"He's a good drum, my lord, but a naughty orator.","sourceLine":"5.3.283","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:5.3:92"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Do you know he promised me marriage?","sourceLine":"5.3.284","speaker":"DIANA","id":"allswell:5.3:93"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Faith, I know more than I'll speak.","sourceLine":"5.3.285","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:5.3:94"},{"kind":"speech","text":"But wilt thou not speak all thou knowest?","sourceLine":"5.3.286","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:5.3:95"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Yes, so please your majesty. I did go between them,\nas I said; but more than that, he loved her: for\nindeed he was mad for her, and talked of Satan and\nof Limbo and of Furies and I know not what: yet I\nwas in that credit with them at that time that I\nknew of their going to bed, and of other motions,\nas promising her marriage, and things which would\nderive me ill will to speak of; therefore I will not\nspeak what I know.","sourceLine":"5.3.287","speaker":"PAROLLES","id":"allswell:5.3:96"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Thou hast spoken all already, unless thou canst say\nthey are married: but thou art too fine in thy\nevidence; therefore stand aside.\nThis ring, you say, was yours?","sourceLine":"5.3.296","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:5.3:97"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Ay, my good lord.","sourceLine":"5.3.300","speaker":"DIANA","id":"allswell:5.3:98"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Where did you buy it? or who gave it you?","sourceLine":"5.3.301","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:5.3:99"},{"kind":"speech","text":"It was not given me, nor I did not buy it.","sourceLine":"5.3.302","speaker":"DIANA","id":"allswell:5.3:100"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Who lent it you?","sourceLine":"5.3.303","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:5.3:101"},{"kind":"speech","text":"It was not lent me neither.","sourceLine":"5.3.304","speaker":"DIANA","id":"allswell:5.3:102"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Where did you find it, then?","sourceLine":"5.3.305","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:5.3:103"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I found it not.","sourceLine":"5.3.306","speaker":"DIANA","id":"allswell:5.3:104"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If it were yours by none of all these ways,\nHow could you give it him?","sourceLine":"5.3.307","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:5.3:105"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I never gave it him.","sourceLine":"5.3.309","speaker":"DIANA","id":"allswell:5.3:106"},{"kind":"speech","text":"This woman's an easy glove, my lord; she goes off\nand on at pleasure.","sourceLine":"5.3.310","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:5.3:107"},{"kind":"speech","text":"This ring was mine; I gave it his first wife.","sourceLine":"5.3.312","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:5.3:108"},{"kind":"speech","text":"It might be yours or hers, for aught I know.","sourceLine":"5.3.313","speaker":"DIANA","id":"allswell:5.3:109"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Take her away; I do not like her now;\nTo prison with her: and away with him.\nUnless thou tell'st me where thou hadst this ring,\nThou diest within this hour.","sourceLine":"5.3.314","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:5.3:110"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I'll never tell you.","sourceLine":"5.3.318","speaker":"DIANA","id":"allswell:5.3:111"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Take her away.","sourceLine":"5.3.319","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:5.3:112"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I'll put in bail, my liege.","sourceLine":"5.3.320","speaker":"DIANA","id":"allswell:5.3:113"},{"kind":"speech","text":"I think thee now some common customer.","sourceLine":"5.3.321","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:5.3:114"},{"kind":"speech","text":"By Jove, if ever I knew man, 'twas you.","sourceLine":"5.3.322","speaker":"DIANA","id":"allswell:5.3:115"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Wherefore hast thou accused him all this while?","sourceLine":"5.3.323","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:5.3:116"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Because he's guilty, and he is not guilty:\nHe knows I am no maid, and he'll swear to't;\nI'll swear I am a maid, and he knows not.\nGreat king, I am no strumpet, by my life;\nI am either maid, or else this old man's wife.","sourceLine":"5.3.324","speaker":"DIANA","id":"allswell:5.3:117"},{"kind":"speech","text":"She does abuse our ears: to prison with her.","sourceLine":"5.3.329","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:5.3:118"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Good mother, fetch my bail. Stay, royal sir:","sourceLine":"5.3.330","speaker":"DIANA","id":"allswell:5.3:119"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exit Widow","id":"allswell:5.3:120"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The jeweller that owes the ring is sent for,\nAnd he shall surety me. But for this lord,\nWho hath abused me, as he knows himself,\nThough yet he never harm'd me, here I quit him:\nHe knows himself my bed he hath defiled;\nAnd at that time he got his wife with child:\nDead though she be, she feels her young one kick:\nSo there's my riddle: one that's dead is quick:\nAnd now behold the meaning.","sourceLine":"5.3.331","speaker":"DIANA","id":"allswell:5.3:121"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Re-enter Widow, with HELENA","id":"allswell:5.3:122"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Is there no exorcist\nBeguiles the truer office of mine eyes?\nIs't real that I see?","sourceLine":"5.3.340","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:5.3:123"},{"kind":"speech","text":"No, my good lord;\n'Tis but the shadow of a wife you see,\nThe name and not the thing.","sourceLine":"5.3.343","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:5.3:124"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Both, both. O, pardon!","sourceLine":"5.3.346","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:5.3:125"},{"kind":"speech","text":"O my good lord, when I was like this maid,\nI found you wondrous kind. There is your ring;\nAnd, look you, here's your letter; this it says:\n'When from my finger you can get this ring\nAnd are by me with child,' & c. This is done:\nWill you be mine, now you are doubly won?","sourceLine":"5.3.347","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:5.3:126"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If she, my liege, can make me know this clearly,\nI'll love her dearly, ever, ever dearly.","sourceLine":"5.3.353","speaker":"BERTRAM","id":"allswell:5.3:127"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If it appear not plain and prove untrue,\nDeadly divorce step between me and you!\nO my dear mother, do I see you living?","sourceLine":"5.3.355","speaker":"HELENA","id":"allswell:5.3:128"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Mine eyes smell onions; I shall weep anon:","sourceLine":"5.3.358","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:5.3:129"},{"kind":"stage","text":"To PAROLLES","id":"allswell:5.3:130"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Good Tom Drum, lend me a handkercher: so,\nI thank thee: wait on me home, I'll make sport with thee:\nLet thy courtesies alone, they are scurvy ones.","sourceLine":"5.3.359","speaker":"LAFEU","id":"allswell:5.3:131"},{"kind":"speech","text":"Let us from point to point this story know,\nTo make the even truth in pleasure flow.","sourceLine":"5.3.362","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:5.3:132"},{"kind":"stage","text":"To DIANA","id":"allswell:5.3:133"},{"kind":"speech","text":"If thou be'st yet a fresh uncropped flower,\nChoose thou thy husband, and I'll pay thy dower;\nFor I can guess that by thy honest aid\nThou keep'st a wife herself, thyself a maid.\nOf that and all the progress, more or less,\nResolvedly more leisure shall express:\nAll yet seems well; and if it end so meet,\nThe bitter past, more welcome is the sweet.","sourceLine":"5.3.364","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:5.3:134"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Flourish","id":"allswell:5.3:135"},{"kind":"speech","text":"EPILOGUE","sourceLine":"5.3.372","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:5.3:136"},{"kind":"speech","text":"The king's a beggar, now the play is done:\nAll is well ended, if this suit be won,\nThat you express content; which we will pay,\nWith strife to please you, day exceeding day:\nOurs be your patience then, and yours our parts;\nYour gentle hands lend us, and take our hearts.","sourceLine":"5.3.373","speaker":"KING","id":"allswell:5.3:137"},{"kind":"stage","text":"Exeunt","id":"allswell:5.3:138"}],"scene":3}],"sourceLineCount":2925,"sourceLabel":"MIT Shakespeare","sectionCount":23,"blockCount":1093,"wordCount":22991};
